          <rect x="597.2" y="392" width="108" height="58" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#D6DAE0" stroke-width="1.2"/>
          <text x="651.2" y="417" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Segoe UI,Arial" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" fill="#15171A">UNIT 360</text>
          <text x="651.2" y="432" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Segoe UI,Arial" font-size="7.4" fill="#4A4F57">FLASH GAS</text></g><text x="161.8" y="307" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Consolas,monospace" font-size="7.4" fill="#4A4F57" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="3.6" paint-order="stroke">F0 · 16.6 MMSCFD</text><text x="273.2" y="307" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Consolas,monospace" font-size="7.4" fill="#4A4F57" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="3.6" paint-order="stroke">F1 · 16.6 MMSCFD</text><text x="424.4" y="307" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Consolas,monospace" font-size="7.4" fill="#4A4F57" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="3.6" paint-order="stroke">F5 · 15.1 MMSCFD</text><text x="575.5999999999999" y="307" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Consolas,monospace" font-size="7.4" fill="#4A4F57" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="3.6" paint-order="stroke">F16 · 16.5 MMSCFD</text><text x="726.8" y="307" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Consolas,monospace" font-size="7.4" fill="#4A4F57" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="3.6" paint-order="stroke">F30 · 14.7 MMSCFD</text><text x="20" y="464" font-family="Consolas,monospace" font-size="7.5" fill="#4A4F57">OVERVIEW · 33 LINKS IN 6 TRUNKS + 9 AS OFF-PAGE TAG + 49 RELIEF LINES FROM 11 UNITS ON THE HEADER RAKE · DASH = AGGREGATE, NOT A PIPE · HMB C1W · utilities &amp; drains hidden</text></svg></div><div class="hmb" style="margin-top:8px;"><div class="hcard out"><div class="hh"><span>&#9654; DUTY &#183; what the unit delivers</span><span>HMB C1</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">Refrigeration, total</span><span class="v">4 751 kW</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">to E-354 &#183; U350</span><span class="v">4 318 kW &#183; 91 %</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">to E-311 &#183; U310</span><span class="v">433 kW &#183; 9 %</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Ammonia circulated</span><span class="v">17 283 kg/h</span></div></div></div><div class="hcard mid"><div class="hh"><span>&#9672; THE LOOP &#183; design case</span><span>R1 &#8594; R2 &#8594; R3</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">Suction &#183; R1</span><span class="v">3.62 barg &#183; 1.25 &#176;C</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Discharge &#183; R2</span><span class="v">25.43 barg &#183; 88 &#176;C</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Condensing &#183; R3</span><span class="v">25.31 barg &#183; 57 &#176;C</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Loop design</span><span class="v">28 barg &#183; PSV-3401A/B</span></div></div></div><div class="hcard in"><div class="hh"><span>&#9654; FOLLOWING DEMAND</span><span>3 working + 1 standby</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">Capacity master</span><span class="v">PIC-3552 &#183; from U350</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Machine capacity</span><span class="v">slide valve 20&#8230;100 %</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Head pressure</span><span class="v">PIC-3401 &#8594; E-342 fans</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">User levels</span><span class="v">LV-3111 / LV-3540</span></div></div></div></div><div class="scrollcue"><span>&#9662; scroll for the figures</span></div></section>
<section class="slide m103safe" data-min="5" data-block="Block 1" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Slow down here. This is the slide that justifies every interlock in the rest of the module.", "Ammonia is an excellent refrigerant &#8212; large latent heat, cheap, and it leaves no residue in the process &#8212; but it is toxic and corrosive, and vapour causes severe irritation or injury to eyes, skin and the respiratory system.", "The design answer is containment: a closed loop built to EN 378, with no ammonia inventory outside the loop. That is why any loss of containment counts as a significant hazard rather than a nuisance.", "The operating answer is simple and must be said exactly: on leakage, evacuate the area immediately and follow the emergency response procedure. Do not attempt to isolate through a cloud.", "Flaring ammonia is an emergency measure only, not a routine disposal route.", "This slide is the principle; the next two are the evidence. Slide 09 is the safety data sheet &#8212; identity, classification, exposure limits and the physical numbers &#8212; and slide 10 is what exposure does, what first aid is, what PPE is correct and how a leak is answered."]'><div class="kicker">Safety &#183; the fluid <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual &#167;7.7 header &#183; EN 378 &#183; NH&#8323; MSDS &#183; TAM-158 NH&#8323; HSE deck</span></div><h2>Ammonia &#8212; the refrigerant and the hazard</h2><div class="symrow big"><div class="ghs"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 3 97 50 50 97 3 50Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M50 26c-9.5 0-17 7-17 15.8 0 5.2 2.7 8.8 5.7 10.8v5.2h22.6v-5.2c3-2 5.7-5.6 5.7-10.8C67 33 59.5 26 50 26Z"/><circle cx="43.2" cy="42.6" r="3.9" fill="#fff"/><circle cx="56.8" cy="42.6" r="3.9" fill="#fff"/><path d="M50 47.5 47.4 53h5.2Z" fill="#fff"/><g stroke="#111" stroke-width="5.4" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M33 63 67 78"/><path d="M67 63 33 78"/></g></g></svg><b>toxic if inhaled<br>H331</b></div><div class="ghs"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 3 97 50 50 97 3 50Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M25.5 27.5 35 32l-8.4 19-9.5-4.5Z"/><path d="M74.5 27.5 65 32l8.4 19 9.5-4.5Z"/><circle cx="27" cy="58" r="2.6"/><circle cx="73" cy="58" r="2.6"/><path d="M14 68h30l-4 7H18Z"/><path d="M56 75c2-5 7-8 13-8h17v8Z"/><path d="M22 62h4v4h-4Zm8 2h3v3h-3Z"/></g></svg><b>severe burns<br>H314</b></div><div class="ghs"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 3 97 50 50 97 3 50Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><rect x="41" y="30" width="18" height="48" rx="8"/><rect x="45.5" y="20" width="9" height="12" rx="2"/><rect x="38" y="26" width="24" height="5" rx="2.5"/></g></svg><b>under pressure<br>H280</b></div><div class="ghs"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 3 97 50 50 97 3 50Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M52 22c1 9-2 13-6 18-4 4.6-8 9-8 16a14 14 0 0 0 28 0c0-8-6-13-8-19-2 4-4 6-6 7 1-8 1-15 0-22Z"/><rect x="24" y="76" width="52" height="5" rx="2.5"/></g></svg><b>flammable gas<br>H221</b></div><div class="ghs"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 3 97 50 50 97 3 50Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M20 74h60" stroke="#111" stroke-width="4.5" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M30 55c9-9 24-9 31 0-7 9-22 9-31 0Z"/><path d="M61 55 74 46v18Z"/><g stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M38 52 42.5 57"/><path d="M42.5 52 38 57"/></g><path d="M28 40 20 26h5l-3-8h11l-3 8h5Z"/></g></svg><b>toxic to water<br>H410</b></div></div><div class="redline"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 6 96 88H4Z" fill="#F4C320" stroke="#111" stroke-width="7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M50 30c-8 0-14 6-14 13 0 4.4 2.3 7.4 4.8 9.1V56h18.4v-3.9c2.5-1.7 4.8-4.7 4.8-9.1 0-7-6-13-14-13Z"/><circle cx="44.4" cy="43.6" r="3.2" fill="#F4C320"/><circle cx="55.6" cy="43.6" r="3.2" fill="#F4C320"/><g stroke="#111" stroke-width="4.4" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M38 61 62 72"/><path d="M62 61 38 72"/></g></g></svg><span>Any loss of containment is a significant hazard &#8212; evacuate first, then follow the emergency response procedure.<i>Do not try to isolate through a cloud &#183; ammonia is toxic and corrosive to eyes, skin and airways</i></span></div><div class="blks c4"><div class="blk grn"><b>Why ammonia</b><ul><li>High latent heat per kg</li><li>No residue in the process gas</li><li>Standard industrial refrigerant</li></ul></div><div class="blk blu"><b>Where it is here</b><ul><li>Vapour: suction and discharge</li><li>Liquid: V-343 and the feed lines</li><li>Boiling: shells of E-311, E-354</li></ul></div><div class="blk amb"><b>Containment</b><ul><li>Closed loop, <b>EN 378</b></li><li><b>28 barg</b> design pressure</li><li>No inventory outside the loop</li></ul></div><div class="blk red"><b>If it escapes</b><ul><li>Evacuate, then report</li><li>Detection <b>2ooN &#8594; PSD</b> &#183; XAHH-3401</li><li>Flare is emergency only, never routine</li></ul></div></div><div class="symrow"><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="6" y="6" width="88" height="88" rx="8" fill="#2E7D46"/><g fill="#111"><circle cx="38" cy="26" r="7" fill="#fff"/><path d="M45 38 34 44l-8 14 6 4 6-10 5 6-6 18 7 3 8-22-7-8 9-4 3 9 7-2-5-14Z" fill="#fff"/><path d="M70 22h14v56H70" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="6"/></g></svg><b>evacuate first<br>then report</b></div><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="6" y="6" width="88" height="88" rx="8" fill="#2E7D46"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M50 18v14" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M26 34h48l-8 10H34Z" fill="#fff"/><g stroke="#fff" stroke-width="4.6" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M36 52v10"/><path d="M50 52v16"/><path d="M64 52v10"/><path d="M43 68v8"/><path d="M57 68v8"/></g></g></svg><b>shower + spill kit<br>E-342-4</b></div><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="6" y="6" width="88" height="88" rx="8" fill="#2E7D46"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M20 56c8-11 22-16 30-16s22 5 30 16c-8 11-22 16-30 16S28 67 20 56Z" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="6"/><circle cx="50" cy="56" r="8" fill="#fff"/><path d="M50 22v10" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/></g></svg><b>eyewash<br>E-342-4</b></div><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="46" fill="#0B5FA5"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M44 26c-12 0-20 7-20 17 0 11 8 20 20 27 12-7 20-16 20-27 0-10-8-17-20-17Z" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="6"/><circle cx="44" cy="48" r="7" fill="#fff"/><rect x="70" y="30" width="14" height="44" rx="7" fill="#fff"/><path d="M64 40h8" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/></g></svg><b>air set before<br>any entry</b></div><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="42" fill="#fff"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M53 30c1 8-2 11-5 15s-6 7-6 12a11 11 0 0 0 22 0c0-7-5-11-7-16-1.6 3-3 4.6-4.6 5.4.8-6 .8-11.4.6-16.4Z"/></g><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="42" fill="none" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="10"/><path d="M22 78 78 22" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="10" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><b>no ignition source<br>near a release</b></div><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 6 96 88H4Z" fill="#F4C320" stroke="#111" stroke-width="7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M50 30c-8 0-14 6-14 13 0 4.4 2.3 7.4 4.8 9.1V56h18.4v-3.9c2.5-1.7 4.8-4.7 4.8-9.1 0-7-6-13-14-13Z"/><circle cx="44.4" cy="43.6" r="3.2" fill="#F4C320"/><circle cx="55.6" cy="43.6" r="3.2" fill="#F4C320"/><g stroke="#111" stroke-width="4.4" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M38 61 62 72"/><path d="M62 61 38 72"/></g></g></svg><b>gas detection<br>GD-05&#8230;09</b></div></div><div class="note">Extinguishers at <b>E-342-1</b> and <b>PK-341-1</b>. Detail: <b>NH&#8323; MSDS</b> + <b>TAM-158 NH&#8323; HSE deck</b>; charging follows <b>QD23-HSEQ-FR03A rev 00</b>. Symbols on this slide are schematic GHS / ISO 7010 &#8212; the plant signs are the reference.</div></section>
<section class="slide m103safe" data-min="3" data-block="Block 1" data-presenter="" data-notes='["These two slides are the NH&#8323; safety data sheet and the TAM-158 HSE deck compressed to what an operator has to carry without looking anything up.", "Identity first: CAS 7664-41-7, EC 231-635-3, UN 1005. Those are how you confirm the sheet in your hand describes the fluid in front of you, and they are what the emergency services will ask for.", "Read the two exposure limits out loud: 20 ppm averaged over eight hours, 50 ppm over fifteen minutes. Detector set points, permits and rescue decisions are measured against those numbers &#8212; not against what anyone can smell.", "Then the four physical numbers that explain the whole plant. It boils at minus 33, so a liquid release refrigerates itself and burns by cold as well as by chemistry. Vapour pressure is 8.6 bar absolute at 20 degrees and 20 bar at 50, which is the reason the loop is designed to 28 barg and the reason containers are kept below 50 degrees. It is flammable between 15.4 and 33.6 percent and needs 630 degrees to self-ignite &#8212; hard to light, but in a closed room it will. And it dissolves at 517 grams per litre, which is why a water spray knocks a cloud down.", "Finish on the open point. The data sheet says the odour threshold is subjective and inadequate to warn of overexposure; refrigeration practice says you smell ammonia long before it hurts you. Both are true, and relying on the second is how people get hurt &#8212; at high concentration the sense of smell fails within minutes. The nose starts the alarm; the detectors decide."]'><div class="kicker">Safety &#183; the data sheet <span class="sub">&#8212; NH&#8323; MSDS scheda 002 &#183; REACH 1907/2006 &#183; CLP 1272/2008 &#183; 13 Dec 2021</span></div><h2>Ammonia &#8212; reading the safety data sheet</h2><div class="facts" style="margin-top:4px;"><div class="fact"><div class="n">7664-41-7</div><div class="l">CAS &#183; EC 231-635-3 &#183; UN 1005 &#183; ADR 2.3 (8) code 2TC</div></div><div class="fact"><div class="n">20 ppm</div><div class="l">8 h occupational exposure limit &#183; 14 mg/m&#179;</div></div><div class="fact"><div class="n">50 ppm</div><div class="l">15 min short-term limit &#183; 36 mg/m&#179;</div></div><div class="fact"><div class="n">&#8722;33 &#176;C</div><div class="l">boiling point &#183; a liquid release self-refrigerates to this</div></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><div class="redline"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 6 96 88H4Z" fill="#F4C320" stroke="#111" stroke-width="7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M50 30c-8 0-14 6-14 13 0 4.4 2.3 7.4 4.8 9.1V56h18.4v-3.9c2.5-1.7 4.8-4.7 4.8-9.1 0-7-6-13-14-13Z"/><circle cx="44.4" cy="43.6" r="3.2" fill="#F4C320"/><circle cx="55.6" cy="43.6" r="3.2" fill="#F4C320"/><g stroke="#111" stroke-width="4.4" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M38 61 62 72"/><path d="M62 61 38 72"/></g></g></svg><span>Signal word: DANGER<i>CLP 1272/2008 &#183; the label on every ammonia container</i></span></div><div class="symrow"><div class="ghs"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 3 97 50 50 97 3 50Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M50 26c-9.5 0-17 7-17 15.8 0 5.2 2.7 8.8 5.7 10.8v5.2h22.6v-5.2c3-2 5.7-5.6 5.7-10.8C67 33 59.5 26 50 26Z"/><circle cx="43.2" cy="42.6" r="3.9" fill="#fff"/><circle cx="56.8" cy="42.6" r="3.9" fill="#fff"/><path d="M50 47.5 47.4 53h5.2Z" fill="#fff"/><g stroke="#111" stroke-width="5.4" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M33 63 67 78"/><path d="M67 63 33 78"/></g></g></svg><b>H331</b></div><div class="ghs"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 3 97 50 50 97 3 50Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M25.5 27.5 35 32l-8.4 19-9.5-4.5Z"/><path d="M74.5 27.5 65 32l8.4 19 9.5-4.5Z"/><circle cx="27" cy="58" r="2.6"/><circle cx="73" cy="58" r="2.6"/><path d="M14 68h30l-4 7H18Z"/><path d="M56 75c2-5 7-8 13-8h17v8Z"/><path d="M22 62h4v4h-4Zm8 2h3v3h-3Z"/></g></svg><b>H314</b></div><div class="ghs"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 3 97 50 50 97 3 50Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><rect x="41" y="30" width="18" height="48" rx="8"/><rect x="45.5" y="20" width="9" height="12" rx="2"/><rect x="38" y="26" width="24" height="5" rx="2.5"/></g></svg><b>H280</b></div><div class="ghs"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 3 97 50 50 97 3 50Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M52 22c1 9-2 13-6 18-4 4.6-8 9-8 16a14 14 0 0 0 28 0c0-8-6-13-8-19-2 4-4 6-6 7 1-8 1-15 0-22Z"/><rect x="24" y="76" width="52" height="5" rx="2.5"/></g></svg><b>H221</b></div><div class="ghs"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 3 97 50 50 97 3 50Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M20 74h60" stroke="#111" stroke-width="4.5" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M30 55c9-9 24-9 31 0-7 9-22 9-31 0Z"/><path d="M61 55 74 46v18Z"/><g stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M38 52 42.5 57"/><path d="M42.5 52 38 57"/></g><path d="M28 40 20 26h5l-3-8h11l-3 8h5Z"/></g></svg><b>H410</b></div></div><div class="chips"><span class="chip"><b>H221</b>flammable gas</span><span class="chip"><b>H280</b>under pressure, may explode if heated</span><span class="chip"><b>H331</b>toxic if inhaled</span><span class="chip"><b>H314</b>severe skin burns and eye damage</span><span class="chip"><b>H410</b>very toxic to aquatic life, long-lasting</span><span class="chip"><b>EUH071</b>corrosive to the respiratory tract</span></div><div class="blks c2"><div class="blk amb"><b>Seveso III</b><ul><li>Named substance, <b>2012/18/EU</b></li><li>Major-accident duty on this inventory</li></ul></div><div class="blk blu"><b>Water</b><ul><li>LC50 96 h, fish <b>0.89 mg/l</b></li><li>Firewater and washings never to drain</li></ul></div></div></div><div><table class="data" style="font-size:10.6px;line-height:1.2;"><tr><th>Property &#183; MSDS &#167;9</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Boiling point &#183; melting point</td><td><b>&#8722;33 &#176;C</b> &#183; &#8722;77.7 &#176;C</td></tr><tr><td>Vapour pressure</td><td><b>8.6 bar(a)</b> at 20 &#176;C &#183; <b>20 bar(a)</b> at 50 &#176;C</td></tr><tr><td>Relative density, gas (air = 1)</td><td>0.6 &#8212; lighter than air</td></tr><tr><td>Relative density, liquid (water = 1)</td><td>0.7</td></tr><tr><td>Flammability limits in air</td><td><b>15.4 &#8211; 33.6 vol %</b></td></tr><tr><td>Autoignition temperature</td><td>630 &#176;C</td></tr><tr><td>Critical temperature</td><td>132 &#176;C &#183; molar mass 17 g/mol</td></tr><tr><td>Solubility in water</td><td><b>517 g/l</b> &#8212; why water spray works</td></tr><tr><td>Acute toxicity, inhalation</td><td>LC50 rat <b>2 000 ppm / 4 h</b></td></tr></table></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b><b>A-27 &#183; the smell is not a measurement.</b> MSDS &#167;9.1 calls the odour threshold <i>inadequate to warn of overexposure</i>; refrigeration practice says you smell it early. Both hold &#8212; at high concentration the nose stops warning within minutes. A smell starts an alarm, <b>XAHH-3401</b> measures one.</div></section>
<section class="slide m103safe" data-min="3" data-block="Block 1" data-presenter="" data-notes='["This is the medical and PPE slide. It is the one the room will remember if it ever matters, so do not rush it.", "The dose ladder, from the TAM-158 toxicology material: low exposure gives cough, rhinitis and conjunctivitis; medium gives acute bronchitis with bronchospasm; high gives laryngeal oedema, asphyxia, massive pulmonary oedema and death. Liquid on skin burns twice &#8212; chemically, and by cold, because a release in air is at minus 33.", "Say the delayed-injury point twice. The MSDS warns that prolonged exposure to a small concentration can produce pulmonary oedema hours after the event, so a casualty who says they feel fine is still a casualty and stays under observation. The sheet also asks for a corticosteroid spray after inhalation as soon as possible &#8212; that is a medical instruction, and it is on the sheet, so the medic should not have to find it.", "Rinse times are not negotiable: eyes and skin, at least fifteen minutes, starting immediately. Know where the shower at E-342-4 is before the day you need it.", "PPE, in one sentence each. The type K green filter is the correct filter for ammonia and it is only valid when the gas is known, oxygen is above 16 percent, concentration is below 2 percent and the job is under 30 minutes &#8212; it does nothing at all about oxygen deficiency. Everything outside that envelope is open-circuit breathing apparatus.", "Leak response is four verbs: evacuate, stay upwind, kill ignition sources, then stop the leak only if it can be reached without risk. Water spray knocks vapour down; a water jet does not. Carbon dioxide is not a suitable medium on ammonia, and a burning leak is left burning until the leak itself can be stopped. Field confirmation, if anyone asks: HCl-soaked cotton gives white NH4Cl fumes and damp litmus turns blue &#8212; both CONFIRM a leak, only XAHH-3401 measures one."]'><div class="kicker">Safety &#183; exposure, PPE and response <span class="sub">&#8212; NH&#8323; MSDS &#167;4 / &#167;5 / &#167;6 / &#167;8 &#183; TAM-158 NH&#8323; HSE deck</span></div><h2>Ammonia exposure &#8212; first aid, PPE and leak response</h2><div class="dose"><div class="lo"><em>Low dose</em><p>Cough &#183; rhinitis &#183; conjunctivitis</p></div><div class="md"><em>Medium dose</em><p>Acute bronchitis with bronchospasm</p></div><div class="hi"><em>High dose</em><p>Laryngeal oedema &#183; asphyxia &#183; pulmonary oedema &#183; death</p></div></div><div class="redline"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 6 96 88H4Z" fill="#F4C320" stroke="#111" stroke-width="7" stroke-linejoin="round"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M50 30c-8 0-14 6-14 13 0 4.4 2.3 7.4 4.8 9.1V56h18.4v-3.9c2.5-1.7 4.8-4.7 4.8-9.1 0-7-6-13-14-13Z"/><circle cx="44.4" cy="43.6" r="3.2" fill="#F4C320"/><circle cx="55.6" cy="43.6" r="3.2" fill="#F4C320"/><g stroke="#111" stroke-width="4.4" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M38 61 62 72"/><path d="M62 61 38 72"/></g></g></svg><span>A casualty who says they feel fine is still a casualty.<i>Small concentrations can bring on pulmonary oedema HOURS later &#8212; observation and medical help &#183; corticosteroid spray after inhalation, as soon as possible</i></span></div><div class="blks c3"><div class="blk grn"><b><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="6" y="6" width="88" height="88" rx="8" fill="#2E7D46"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M42 22h16v20h20v16H58v20H42V58H22V42h20Z" fill="#fff"/></g></svg>Inhalation</b><ul><li>Clean air &#8212; <b>you</b> wear the air set</li><li>Lying down, warm, call a doctor</li><li>Artificial respiration only if breathing stopped</li></ul></div><div class="blk grn"><b><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="6" y="6" width="88" height="88" rx="8" fill="#2E7D46"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M50 18v14" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M26 34h48l-8 10H34Z" fill="#fff"/><g stroke="#fff" stroke-width="4.6" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M36 52v10"/><path d="M50 52v16"/><path d="M64 52v10"/><path d="M43 68v8"/><path d="M57 68v8"/></g></g></svg>Skin &#183; liquid burns twice</b><ul><li>Strip contaminated clothing</li><li>Wash <b>15 min</b> minimum</li><li>Cold burn at &#8722;33 &#176;C: spray, sterile dressing</li></ul></div><div class="blk grn"><b><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="6" y="6" width="88" height="88" rx="8" fill="#2E7D46"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M20 56c8-11 22-16 30-16s22 5 30 16c-8 11-22 16-30 16S28 67 20 56Z" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="6"/><circle cx="50" cy="56" r="8" fill="#fff"/><path d="M50 22v10" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/></g></svg>Eyes</b><ul><li>Rinse <b>15 min</b> minimum</li><li>Immediately &#8212; do not walk to find help</li><li>Eyewash and shower at <b>E-342-4</b></li></ul></div></div><div class="symrow"><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="46" fill="#0B5FA5"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M50 24c-14 0-24 8-24 19 0 12 9 22 24 30 15-8 24-18 24-30 0-11-10-19-24-19Z" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="6"/><circle cx="50" cy="52" r="8" fill="#fff"/><path d="M31 40h38" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/></g></svg><b>K &#183; green filter<br>only in the envelope</b></div><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="46" fill="#0B5FA5"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M44 26c-12 0-20 7-20 17 0 11 8 20 20 27 12-7 20-16 20-27 0-10-8-17-20-17Z" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="6"/><circle cx="44" cy="48" r="7" fill="#fff"/><rect x="70" y="30" width="14" height="44" rx="7" fill="#fff"/><path d="M64 40h8" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="5" stroke-linecap="round"/></g></svg><b>SCBA EN 137<br>everything else</b></div><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="46" fill="#0B5FA5"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M38 22h24l14 10-6 12-6-3v37H30V41l-6 3-6-12Z" fill="#fff"/><path d="M50 32v46" stroke="#0B5FA5" stroke-width="3.5"/></g></svg><b>gas-tight<br>EN 943</b></div><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="46" fill="#0B5FA5"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M34 78V44c0-4 6-4 6 0v-8c0-4 6-4 6 0v-4c0-4 6-4 6 0v6c0-4 6-4 6 0v10c4 0 8 2 8 8v22Z" fill="#fff"/></g></svg><b>EN 374 &#183; EN 511<br>chemical &#183; cryogenic</b></div><div class="sgn"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="46" fill="#0B5FA5"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M20 44h60v8a14 14 0 0 1-14 14H34a14 14 0 0 1-14-14Z" fill="#fff"/><path d="M50 50v14" stroke="#0B5FA5" stroke-width="4"/><path d="M14 40h72" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/></g></svg><b>goggles + shield<br>EN 166</b></div></div><div class="blks c2"><div class="blk blu"><b>The filter envelope &#8212; all four, or no filter</b><ul><li>Gas <b>known</b> &#183; O&#8322; <b>&gt; 16 %</b></li><li>Concentration <b>&lt; 2 %</b> &#183; job <b>&lt; 30 min</b></li></ul></div><div class="blk red"><b>Outside the envelope</b><ul><li>A filter does nothing about oxygen deficiency</li><li>Confined or unknown atmosphere: <b>SCBA only</b></li></ul></div></div><div class="steps"><div><u>1</u><p><b>Evacuate</b> the area</p></div><div><u>2</u><p>Stay <b>upwind</b></p></div><div><u>3</u><p>Kill <b>ignition sources</b></p></div><div><u>4</u><p>Stop the leak <b>only if reachable without risk</b></p></div></div><div class="never"><div><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="42" fill="#fff"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M22 44h26l26-10v32L48 56H22Z"/><path d="M78 40h8M78 50h10M78 60h8" stroke="#111" stroke-width="4" stroke-linecap="round"/></g><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="42" fill="none" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="10"/><path d="M22 78 78 22" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="10" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><span>Knock vapour down with <b>water spray</b> &#8212; never a jet</span></div><div><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="42" fill="#fff"/><g fill="#111"><path d="M53 30c1 8-2 11-5 15s-6 7-6 12a11 11 0 0 0 22 0c0-7-5-11-7-16-1.6 3-3 4.6-4.6 5.4.8-6 .8-11.4.6-16.4Z"/></g><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="42" fill="none" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="10"/><path d="M22 78 78 22" stroke="#C8102E" stroke-width="10" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg><span><b>CO&#8322; is not</b> a suitable medium &#183; a burning leak is left burning until the leak can be stopped</span></div></div></section>
<section class="slide m340cyc" data-min="6" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Build the cycle from scratch, because the rest of the module is just this drawing with more detail on it.", "Four steps and one fluid. Compress the vapour so it can condense against ambient air; condense it and give up the heat; expand it through a valve so it becomes cold; boil it in the exchanger where the cooling is wanted. Then do it again.", "The teaching point most people miss: the cooling comes from BOILING, not from cold liquid arriving. That is why both evaporators are flooded and level-controlled &#8212; the liquid level is the heat-transfer surface.", "The pressure story is just as short: the loop has a high side and a low side, and the throttling point at each user is the border between them. On the design case the low side is around 3.6 barg and the high side around 25.4 barg.", "The figure is Manual Fig. 7.8, the R1 loop as the manual draws it, and it is deliberately the whole slide &#8212; work on the drawing, not on the text. Click it for the complete sheet, with the fluid list and the title block.", "Nothing on this slide is control. How capacity follows suction pressure and how the fans hold head pressure are Block 4; the slide valve itself is Block 3. If the room asks here, park it &#8212; the answer is coming.", "Compare this sheet with our own on the next slide &#8212; same loop, different level of detail."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 1/12 &#183; the loop at a glance <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual &#167;7.4 &#183; Fig. 7.8 &#183; single-stage, closed, EN 378</span></div><h2>Unit 340 &#8212; Ammonia Loop</h2><div class="cycfig"><img src="/images/340-R1-loop-tight.png" data-zoom-src="/images/340-R1-loop-manual.png" data-zoom-cap="Manual Fig. 7.8 &#8212; the complete R1 sheet, with the fluid list and the title block" alt="R1 refrigerant loop as drawn in the Operating Manual, Fig. 7.8"></div><div class="cyccards"><div class="step"><div class="u">&#9312; COMPRESS</div><div class="t"><b>C-341-x</b> &#183; oil-flooded screw</div><div class="p">3.6 &#8594; 25.4 barg &#183; 4 &#215; 33 %</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">&#9313; CONDENSE</div><div class="t"><b>E-342-1&#8230;6</b> &#183; air-cooled</div><div class="p">57 &#176;C &#183; 25.31 barg &#183; vapour &#8594; liquid</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">&#9314; COLLECT</div><div class="t"><b>V-343</b> &#183; liquid buffer</div><div class="p">28 barg design &#183; feeds both users</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">&#9315; EXPAND &amp; EVAPORATE</div><div class="t"><b>E-311</b> &#183; <b>E-354</b> &#183; throttled, boils</div><div class="p">back to suction 3.6 barg &#183; case C1S</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="7" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["This is the master drawing of the module. Every equipment slide in Block 2 shows this same geometry with one machine lit &#8212; nothing is redrawn, so what you learn here transfers directly.", "Walk it once with a finger before saying anything else: suction header into the packages, discharge through PCV-3401 to the condensers, condensed liquid down to V-343, out to the two users, through the level valves, boiled in the shells, and back up the suction header.", "Read it as a PFD, because that is what it now is: the tag is underlined with the service printed under it, every run carries its stream balloon, the pentagons are off-page connectors that name the destination AND the drawing, and the fluid list and title block sit in the bottom corners. Two honest notes are printed on the sheet: fail actions are null in the register for every Unit-340 valve except LV-3111 and LV-3540, and no manual valve of this unit is digitised.", "Read the colours off the legend at the bottom: each colour is a state of the same fluid, not a different fluid. The whole point of a refrigeration cycle is that one substance changes state four times.", "Two controllers are drawn as dashed links because they act at a distance: PIC-3552 on the suction header sets the compressor slide valves, and PIC-3401 on the receiver stages the condenser fans.", "Give the room a minute with this slide. Everything after the break is a zoom into one corner of it."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 2/12 &#183; the whole cycle <span class="sub">&#8212; drawn as a PFD &#183; UFD PRO10-ING-PR07 &#183; P&amp;ID PR38 &#183; the master sheet of this module</span></div><h2>The R1 loop &#8212; one closed cycle</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:6px 10px 3px;"><div class="lbl"><span>Unit 340 &#8212; the R1 ammonia loop, in flow order</span><span class="you">&#9632; one fluid &#183; four states &#183; four steps</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="cycle" data-ann="cycle"></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="4" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Now put numbers on the drawing. These are the heat-and-material-balance streams for design case C1S, taken from the plant database, and they are the only operating numbers in this module that are a balance rather than a set point.", "Read the three that matter. R1 is what comes back to the compressors: saturated vapour at about 1.25 &#176;C and 3.62 barg. R2 is the discharge: 88 &#176;C at 25.43 barg &#8212; that is why the discharge temperature trip sits at 110 &#176;C. R3 is the condensed liquid at 57 &#176;C and 25.31 barg.", "Then the split: about 1 569 kg/h goes to E-311 and about 15 714 kg/h to E-354. E-354 takes roughly ninety per cent of the load, which is why the liquid line to it is 4 inch and the one to E-311 is one and a half inch.", "The stream codes jump: there is no R7, R8 or R9 in the register. Say that plainly rather than letting someone hunt for them &#8212; the loop is complete without them.", "These are design figures for one case. Three more cases exist (C1W, C2S, C2W) with the same structure and slightly different flows."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 3/12 &#183; the cycle in numbers <span class="sub">&#8212; plant_streams &#183; circuit ammonia &#183; design case <b>C1S</b></span></div><h2>The cycle in numbers &#8212; R1&#8230;R12, design case C1S</h2><table class="data" style="font-size:11px;line-height:1.2;"><tr><th>Stream</th><th>What it is</th><th>Vap. frac.</th><th>T &#176;C</th><th>P barg</th><th>kg/h</th><th>kW</th></tr><tr><td><span class="k">R1</span></td><td>Suction vapour returning to PK-341</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.254</td><td>3.620</td><td>17 282.6</td><td>&#8722;13 366.4</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">R2</span></td><td>Compressor discharge to the condensers</td><td>1.0</td><td><b>88.0</b></td><td><b>25.43</b></td><td>17 282.6</td><td>&#8722;12 757.7</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">R3</span></td><td>Condensed liquid, before and after V-343</td><td>0.0</td><td><b>57.0</b></td><td><b>25.31</b></td><td>17 282.6</td><td>&#8722;18 106.1</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">R4</span></td><td>Liquid branch to E-311</td><td>0.0</td><td>57.0</td><td>25.31</td><td>1 568.8</td><td>&#8722;1 643.6</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">R5</span></td><td>After LV-3111 &#8212; flashed, two-phase</td><td>0.191</td><td>8.839</td><td>5.070</td><td>1 568.8</td><td>&#8722;1 643.6</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">R6</span></td><td>E-311 vapour outlet, to PCV-3101</td><td>1.0</td><td>8.595</td><td>5.020</td><td>1 568.8</td><td>&#8722;1 210.3</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">R10</span></td><td>Liquid branch to E-354</td><td>0.0</td><td>57.0</td><td>25.31</td><td>15 713.7</td><td>&#8722;16 462.5</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">R11</span></td><td>After LV-3540 &#8212; flashed, two-phase</td><td>0.213</td><td>1.896</td><td>3.770</td><td>15 713.7</td><td>&#8722;16 462.5</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">R12</span></td><td>E-354 vapour outlet, to the suction header</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.0</td><td>3.620</td><td>15 713.7</td><td>&#8722;12 156.1</td></tr></table><div class="cr340"><b>as-read</b><b>R7, R8 and R9 do not exist</b> in the stream register &#8212; the codes jump from R6 to R10. The loop is complete as listed; the gap is a numbering artefact, not a missing line.</div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="2" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Start the walkthrough at the coldest, lowest-pressure point of the loop, because that is where the compressors take their duty from.", "Both users return vapour to one common header: 3 inch from E-311 and 10 inch from E-354, joining a 12 inch suction header that feeds all four packages through 6 inch spurs.", "The measurement that runs the unit sits here: PT-3552 at the E-354 vapour outlet feeds PIC-3552, and that signal goes to every package. Low suction pressure means the compressors are taking more than the users are giving.", "PCV-3101 on the E-311 vapour outlet is a back-pressure valve, set at 4.5 barg on the P&amp;ID. It keeps E-311 evaporating warmer than E-354 &#8212; deliberately worse cooling, so the gas is not chilled into hydrate territory upstream of the adsorbers.", "The failure mode to name here is liquid carry-over: a compressor takes vapour, not liquid, and the level loops at the two evaporators exist to make sure it only ever gets vapour."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 4/12 &#183; the low side <span class="sub">&#8212; suction header <b>12&quot;-AN-340009-3L1-C35</b> &#183; PR38</span></div><h2>The suction side &#8212; where the vapour comes back</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>The master PFD &#8212; the two vapour returns and the common suction header</span><span class="you">&#9632; suction side &#183; PCV-3101 &#183; PIC-3552</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="cycle" data-hi="SUCTION PCV-3101"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><ul class="points"><li><b>From E-311:</b> <b>3&quot;-AN-000204-3L1-C25</b>, through <b>PCV-3101</b> (3&quot;, set 4.5 barg) and 310-HV-024.</li><li><b>From E-354:</b> <b>10&quot;-AN-000123-3L1-C35</b>, off a 12&quot;&#215;10&quot; reducer at the separator drum, past <b>PT-3552</b> and TT-3554.</li><li><b>Into the packages:</b> 6&quot; spurs 000321 / 000326 / 000331 / 000336, package letters <b>A / E / I / M</b>, each with a check valve and a suction strainer inside the skid.</li></ul></div><div><div class="hcard ioc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9636; What is measured here</span><span>suction</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">Common suction pressure</span><span class="v">PT-3552 &#8594; PIC-3552 &#183; 0..34.47 barg</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Per-machine suction</span><span class="v">PT-3411.x &#183; 0..13.7895 bar abs</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Per-machine suction temp.</span><span class="v">TT-3419.x &#8594; TAHH-3419.x (LSD)</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Design case</span><span class="v">R1 &#183; 1.25 &#176;C &#183; 3.62 barg &#183; 17 283 kg/h</span></div></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="3" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Back to the high side. All four packages discharge into one 8 inch header, and the first thing on that header is PCV-3401.", "PCV-3401 is a 6 inch self-acting regulator set at 25.45 barg, sitting between two manual isolations with a 6 inch manual bypass around it. On PR38 rev 8 it carries NO solenoid and no interlock &#8212; it is purely self-acting. The database holds a pilot PEV-3401 that the drawing does not show.", "PCV-3402 is the smaller partner: one and a half inch, set at 24.7 barg, and it does have a pilot, PEV-3402, marked solenoid on valve open, with an interlock block. Trace it on PR38 and it runs from the far end of the discharge header, through the valve, into receiver nozzle N2 in the vapour space &#8212; the spec break 3C1 to 3L1 sits across the valve, which fixes the direction.", "Read the two together and they look like classic head-pressure control for air-cooled condensers: throttle the feed to the condensers and bypass hot gas to the receiver so liquid keeps flowing when the air is cold. The ROUTING is now traced from the drawing; what is still an inference is the INTENT, because no manual text describes either valve.", "Also flag the data gap: both valves exist in the database with empty set-pressure fields. The 25.45 and 24.7 figures come from the drawing and the HMI, not from the register."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 5/12 &#183; the high side <span class="sub">&#8212; discharge header <b>8&quot;-AN-340004-3C1</b> &#183; PR38 &#183; HMI Fig. 7.2</span></div><h2>The discharge side &#8212; PCV-3401</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>The master PFD &#8212; the discharge header, PCV-3401 and its manual bypass</span><span class="you">&#9632; PCV-3401 &#183; PCV-3402 &#183; the pressure ladder</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="cycle" data-hi="PCV-3401 340-HR-001"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><ul class="points"><li><b>PCV-3401 &#183; 6&quot; &#183; set 25.45 barg</b> in the common discharge header, between <b>340-HV-014</b> and <b>340-HV-013</b>, manual bypass <b>340-HR-001</b>, drains 340-HV-008 / -010 &#183; <b>self-acting, no solenoid on PR38</b>.</li><li><b>PCV-3402 &#183; 1&#189;&quot; &#183; set 24.7 barg</b> from 1&#189;&quot;-AN-340005-3C1 (discharge header) to 1&#189;&quot;-AN-340006-3L1 and <b>V-343 nozzle N2</b>, through 340-HV-015 / -016, pilot <b>PEV-3402</b> (solenoid on valve open).</li></ul></div><div><div class="hcard ioc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9636; The pressure ladder, top down</span><span>barg</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">PSV-3401A/B</span><span class="v"><b>28</b> &#183; relief, fire case</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PAHH-3412.x</span><span class="v"><b>27.5</b> &#183; compressor discharge trip</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PAH-3401</span><span class="v"><b>27</b> &#183; receiver high alarm</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PCV-3401</span><span class="v"><b>25.45</b> &#183; discharge header regulator</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PIC-3401 SP</span><span class="v"><b>25</b> &#183; Manual Table 7.9 (null in the database)</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PCV-3402</span><span class="v"><b>24.7</b> &#183; to the receiver vapour space</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PAL-3401</span><span class="v"><b>20</b> &#183; receiver low alarm</span></div></div></div></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b>The <b>routing</b> of both valves is traced on PR38 rev 8; the <b>head-pressure duty</b> is still an inference, because no manual text describes either valve. Both set pressures are <b>absent from the database</b>, and <b>PEV-3401 is a register record with no counterpart on the drawing</b>.</div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="3" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Six air-cooled condensers in parallel, each rated 5 116 kW. This is where all the heat the loop collects finally leaves the plant.", "Control is deliberately crude and very robust: PIC-3401 measures receiver pressure on PT-3401 and starts or stops fan motors with hysteresis. It is an on/off staging loop, not a modulating one, and the HMI shows a fans-running counter per condenser.", "That is also the operator skill here. On a hot day more fans run and the head pressure sits high; on a cold night fans stop and the hot-gas bypass is understood to keep the receiver pressurised &#8212; that duty is our reading of PR38, not a manual statement. A condenser bank with fewer fans running than its neighbours is worth a look, not an alarm.", "Now the honesty. The fan count per condenser is 12 in the manual, 18 on P&amp;ID PR38 rev 8, and 12 in the database asset register &#8212; while the database equipment note itself says 18. The voltage is 690 V in the manual and 400 V on both the P&amp;ID and the database. Both remain open change requests.", "Do not let a trainee leave believing a number here. Teach them where to look instead: the MCC panels JG-342-1 to JG-342-6 have one starter per fan, and counting starters settles it in the field."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 6/12 &#183; the condensers <span class="sub">&#8212; E-342-1&#8230;6 &#183; PR38 data block &#183; PIC-3401 fan staging</span></div><h2>E-342-1&#8230;6 &#8212; the air-cooled condensers</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>The master PFD &#8212; the condenser bank and the liquid down to V-343</span><span class="you">&#9632; E-342-1&#8230;6 &#183; PIC-3401 fan staging</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="cycle" data-hi="E-342"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><ul class="points"><li><b>Duty 5 116 kW each</b> &#183; design <b>FV/28 barg</b>, &#8722;45/120 &#176;C &#183; 304 tube with aluminium fins &#183; surface <b>3 341 m&#178;</b> (P&amp;ID data block).</li><li><b>PIC-3401</b> holds receiver pressure by <b>starting and stopping fan motors</b> with hysteresis &#183; range 0..28 barg &#183; SP <b>25 barg</b> in Manual Table 7.9.</li><li>Inlets N1A/N1B, outlets N2A/N2B to the collection header <b>6&quot;-AN-340001-3L1</b>; isolation 340-HV-001 to -011 in, 340-HV-02A/B to -12A/B out &#8212; the outlet valves really are numbered with two digits on PR38, so <b>340-HV-02A is not 340-HV-002</b> (which is a 3&quot; PSV outlet).</li><li>Fan motors on MCC panels <b>JG-342-1&#8230;6</b>, one starter per fan, fail position stop.</li></ul></div><div><div class="hcard eqc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9888; What the sources say about the fans</span><span>open CR</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">Manual Tables 7.3 / 7.5</span><span class="v"><b>12</b> fans per condenser &#183; <b>690 V</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">P&amp;ID PR38 rev 8</span><span class="v"><b>18</b> &#8212; in <b>four</b> places, see below</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Database asset register</span><span class="v"><b>12</b> fans per condenser &#183; <b>400 V</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Database equipment note</span><span class="v"><b>18 each</b> &#8212; contradicts its own registry</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Fan motor</span><span class="v">Seipee JM 112Ma 4 &#183; 4 kW &#183; IP65 &#183; DOL</span></div></div></div></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b>PR38 rev 8 says 18 <b>four independent times</b>: data block <b>FAN Qty 18 (each)</b>, fan tag range <b>F-342-1..6/1..18</b>, motor tag range <b>MF-342-1-1/1-18 ... 6-1/6-18</b>, and the note on the condenser itself, <b>1 TO 18 MOTORS TYPICAL</b>. The register&#8217;s 12 rests on 72 asset rows and 12-slot MCC panels. The deck prints the register value and the CR stays open &#8212; count the starters in JG-342-1&#8230;6 to settle it.</div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="3" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["One horizontal receiver serves the whole unit. It is the buffer that lets the two evaporators change load independently, and it is where the refrigerant inventory of the plant actually lives.", "The vendor adds the design intent, and it is the sentence that matters for maintenance: the receiver is sized to hold the COMPLETE refrigerant charge of the system. So the whole loop can be pumped down into V-343 before maintenance work &#8212; that is how you get an ammonia-free vessel or exchanger without venting the plant inventory.", "Two measurements on it define the unit: PT-3401 gives receiver pressure, which is what PIC-3401 controls; LT-3401 gives liquid level, which is your inventory gauge. A slowly falling level with no operational reason means you are losing ammonia somewhere.", "The relief is a pair on a change-over valve with a lock device, set at 28 barg, fire case &#8212; and the change-over is what lets one valve be tested while the vessel stays protected. Never leave the change-over mid-travel.", "Gas detector GD-09 sits over this vessel &#8212; the receiver holds the largest single liquid inventory in the unit, so that placement is not accidental."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 7/12 &#183; the receiver <span class="sub">&#8212; V-343 &#183; PR38 &#183; vendor manual slide 40 &#183; the liquid inventory of the plant</span></div><h2>V-343 &#8212; the ammonia receiver</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>The master PFD &#8212; the receiver, its instruments and its relief route</span><span class="you">&#9632; V-343 &#183; level, pressure and relief</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="cycle" data-hi="V-343 PSV-3401 COV-3401 RELIEF"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><ul class="points"><li><b>Sized to hold the COMPLETE refrigerant charge</b> &#8212; the whole system can be <b>pumped down into V-343</b> for maintenance (vendor manual slide 40).</li><li><b>FV / 28 barg</b>, &#8722;45/120 &#176;C &#183; ID <b>800 mm</b> &#183; TL&#8211;TL <b>5 000 mm</b> &#183; <b>LTCS</b> &#183; internal volume <b>2.5 m&#179;</b> &#183; no insulation.</li><li><b>LLL 180 mm from BTL</b> &#183; liquid outlet nozzle <b>N4</b>, 6&quot; through <b>340-HV-029</b> into <b>6&quot;-AN-340007-3L1</b>, feeding <b>E-311 and E-354</b>.</li><li><b>PSV-3401A / PSV-3401B &#183; 28 barg</b>, fire case, on change-over valve <b>COV-3401</b> with a lock device &#183; drain <b>340-HV-026</b> &#183; gas detector <b>GD-09</b> above it.</li></ul></div><div><div class="hcard ioc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9636; What you watch on V-343</span><span>receiver</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">PT-3401</span><span class="v">0..28 barg &#183; the PIC-3401 measurement</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PAH / PAL-3401</span><span class="v"><b>27</b> / <b>20 barg</b> &#183; alarm only</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">LT-3401</span><span class="v">liquid level &#183; ABB &#183; DP with diaphragm seals</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">LAL-3401</span><span class="v"><b>25 %</b> &#183; low level alarm</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Local</span><span class="v">PG-3402 gauge &#183; sight glasses SG-3401A/B</span></div></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="2" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["From the receiver the liquid goes out on one 6 inch line and splits: 4 inch to E-354 and one and a half inch to E-311. The sizes tell you the duty split before you read a single number.", "On the design case that split is about 15 700 kg/h to E-354 and about 1 570 kg/h to E-311 &#8212; roughly ten to one.", "The expansion happens at the users, not here. LV-3111 and LV-3540 are the level valves on the two shells and they are also the pressure boundary between the high and the low side of the loop.", "Say this clearly because it confuses people every time: both level controllers belong to OTHER units. LIC-3111 is a Unit 310 loop, LIC-3540 is a Unit 350 loop. Unit 340 supplies the liquid; the users decide how much of it they take.", "Both liquid lines are recorded as CONFIRMED process links in the database, with the level valve named on the link &#8212; that is the cleanest interface documentation in this whole unit."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 8/12 &#183; liquid distribution <span class="sub">&#8212; <b>6&quot;-AN-340007-3L1</b> out of V-343 &#183; PR38 &#8594; PR08 / PR22</span></div><h2>Liquid distribution to the two users</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>The master PFD &#8212; the liquid line out of V-343 and the split to the users</span><span class="you">&#9632; LV-3111 &#183; LV-3540</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="cycle" data-hi="DIST 340-HV-029"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><table class="data" style="font-size:10.6px;"><tr><th></th><th>To E-311 (U310)</th><th>To E-354 (U350)</th></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Line</span></td><td>1&#189;&quot;-AN-000201-3L1</td><td>4&quot;-AN-000124-3L1</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Level valve</span></td><td><b>LV-3111</b> &#183; 1&quot;, fail closed</td><td><b>LV-3540</b> &#183; 3&quot;, fail closed</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Controller</span></td><td>LIC-3111 &#8212; <b>Unit 310</b></td><td>LIC-3540 &#8212; <b>Unit 350</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Solenoid</span></td><td>LEV-3111 (ISA 02)</td><td>LEV-3540 (ISA 04)</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Manual bypass</span></td><td>310-HR-005 (1&#189;&quot;)</td><td>354-HR-003 (3&quot;)</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Design flow C1S</span></td><td>1 568.8 kg/h</td><td>15 713.7 kg/h</td></tr></table></div><div><ul class="points"><li><b>The valve is the border.</b> Upstream of LV-3111 / LV-3540 the ammonia is liquid at about 25.3 barg; downstream it is a boiling two-phase mixture at 5.07 and 3.77 barg respectively.</li><li><b>Level is the heat-transfer surface.</b> Too low and the exchanger loses duty; too high and liquid carries over to the compressors.</li><li><b>Neither loop is a Unit 340 loop.</b> The refrigeration operator sees the effect &#8212; suction pressure and receiver level &#8212; not the set point.</li></ul></div></div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="3" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["The smaller of the two users, and the one with the most interesting control problem.", "The vendor names the type: a BXU shell-and-tube exchanger with a U-tube bundle, and it tells you the orientation &#8212; liquid ammonia enters at the BOTTOM and the ammonia vapour leaves the top. Natural gas is in the tubes, boiling ammonia in the shell, with a scrubber drum above so vapour leaves dry.", "The special feature is the back-pressure valve on the vapour outlet, PCV-3101, set at 4.5 barg. By holding the outlet pressure up, it holds the evaporating temperature up, so E-311 runs WARMER than E-354 on purpose. Cool the gas too far ahead of the adsorbers and you form hydrates.", "A separate valve, PCV-3111, 1 inch and set at 4 barg, injects hot gas into the liquid feed just downstream of LV-3111. Two different valves, two different jobs &#8212; the P&amp;ID and the Unit 310 valve register agree on that, and the manual section 7.4 does not.", "Levels: normal 35 per cent with a high alarm at 41 per cent in both the manual and the vendor manual; on the drawing LT-3111 drives the level controller and LSHH-3101 on the drum is an independent high-high that takes a plant shutdown."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 9/12 &#183; user 1 <span class="sub">&#8212; E-311 &#183; <b>BXU U-tube</b> &#183; Unit 310 &#183; P&amp;ID PR08 &#183; vendor manual slide 42</span></div><h2>E-311 &#8212; the natural gas precooler</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>The master PFD &#8212; the E-311 branch, its level valve and PCV-3101</span><span class="you">&#9632; E-311 &#183; level, back-pressure, hot gas</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="cycle" data-hi="E-311 LV-3111 PCV-3101"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><ul class="points"><li><b>BXU shell-and-tube with a U-tube bundle</b> &#8212; <b>liquid ammonia enters at the BOTTOM, vapour leaves the top</b> (vendor). Kettle plus scrubber drum, 6&quot; risers.</li><li>Duty <b>433 kW</b> &#183; shell 28 barg, &#8722;45/90 &#176;C &#183; tube 50 barg &#183; surface <b>60.8 m&#178;</b> &#183; LTCS.</li><li><b>PCV-3101 &#183; 3&quot; &#183; set 4.5 barg</b> on the vapour outlet with solenoid <b>PEV-3101</b> &#8212; back-pressure, keeps E-311 warmer than E-354 and prevents <b>hydrate formation</b> upstream of the adsorbers.</li><li><b>PCV-3111 &#183; 1&quot; &#183; set 4 barg</b> with solenoid <b>PEV-3111</b> on the hot-gas line, discharging <b>into the liquid feed</b> downstream of LV-3111.</li><li><b>PSV-3101A/B &#183; 28 barg</b>, tube-rupture case, on change-over <b>COV-3101</b> &#8594; the ammonia flare header.</li></ul></div><div><div class="hcard ioc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9636; Level and protection</span><span>E-311</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">LT-3111 &#8594; LIC-3111</span><span class="v">&#8594; LV-3111 &#183; normal <b>35 %</b>, high alarm <b>41 %</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">LAH-3111</span><span class="v">interlock / LSD</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">LSHH-3101</span><span class="v">drum high-high &#8594; <b>LAHH-3101 &#183; PSD</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Sight glasses</span><span class="v">SG-3102A (drum) &#183; SG-3102B/C (shell)</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Levels on the drawing</span><span class="v">HLL 430 &#183; NLL 380 &#183; LLL 50 mm</span></div></div></div></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b>Manual &#167;7.4 attributes the back-pressure duty to <b>PCV-3111</b>. The P&amp;ID, the UFD, &#167;7.7.4 and the Unit 310 valve register all say <b>PCV-3101</b> &#8212; and they are two different valves, not two names for one.</div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="3" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["The big user. E-354 takes about ninety per cent of the refrigeration duty and it is the reason four compressors exist rather than one.", "Same type as E-311 in the vendor manual: a BXU shell-and-tube with a U-tube bundle, liquid ammonia entering at the BOTTOM and vapour leaving the top &#8212; but a much bigger machine, 4 318 kW of duty, four 12 inch risers between shell and drum, and a 10 inch vapour outlet.", "The control master of the whole unit sits on that outlet. PT-3552 measures there and PIC-3552 sends the signal to all four compressor packages. In other words, the Unit 350 evaporator sets the load of Unit 340.", "The hot-gas line arrives here too, PCV-3540 at 2.8 barg, and again it injects into the liquid feed upstream of the shell nozzle &#8212; the same arrangement as at E-311.", "Protection mirrors E-311: LSHH-3541 gives a plant shutdown on high-high level, and PSV-3503A/B at 28 barg, fire case, protect the drum."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 10/12 &#183; user 2 <span class="sub">&#8212; E-354 &#183; <b>BXU U-tube</b> &#183; Unit 350 &#183; P&amp;ID PR22 &#183; vendor manual slide 43</span></div><h2>E-354 &#8212; the ammonia / MR cooler</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>The master PFD &#8212; the E-354 branch and the 10&quot; vapour return</span><span class="you">&#9632; E-354 &#183; LIC-3540 &#183; PT-3552</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="cycle" data-hi="E-354 LV-3540"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><ul class="points"><li><b>BXU shell-and-tube with a U-tube bundle</b> &#8212; <b>liquid ammonia in at the BOTTOM, vapour out at the top</b> (vendor), kettle plus separator drum.</li><li><b>Duty 4 318 kW</b> &#183; cold side FV/28 barg, &#8722;45/90 &#176;C &#183; hot side 28 barg, &#8722;45/120 &#176;C &#183; surface <b>1 040.8 m&#178;</b> &#183; cold side LTCS.</li><li>Shell and drum joined by four <b>12&quot;</b> risers N2A&#8211;N2D; vapour leaves nozzle N5, 10&quot;, through a 12&quot;&#215;10&quot; reducer.</li><li><b>PCV-3540 &#183; 2&quot; &#183; set 2.8 barg</b> on the hot-gas line, into the liquid feed upstream of shell nozzle N1.</li></ul></div><div><div class="hcard ioc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9636; Level, pressure, protection</span><span>E-354</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">LT-3540 &#8594; LIC-3540</span><span class="v">&#8594; LV-3540 &#183; normal level <b>90 %</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PT-3552 &#8594; PIC-3552</span><span class="v">0..34.47 barg &#183; the compressor capacity master</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">LSHH-3541</span><span class="v">&#8594; <b>LAHH-3541 &#183; PSD</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PSV-3503A/B</span><span class="v"><b>28 barg</b>, fire case, on COV-3503</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Local</span><span class="v">LG-3540 DP gauge &#183; SG-3540 &#183; PG-3540</span></div></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="2" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["One short slide to kill a common misconception.", "Hot gas is tapped off the 8 inch discharge header, drops to a 4 inch line at the spec break, and runs to both users: one and a half inch to E-311 and 3 inch to E-354.", "It does NOT go to a defrost header. On both user drawings the hot-gas line ties into the LIQUID FEED just upstream of the evaporator, downstream of the level valve. It is an injection into the feed, controlled by its own regulator: PCV-3111 at 4 barg at E-311, PCV-3540 at 2.8 barg at E-354.", "Head-pressure control is a different pair of valves, PCV-3401 and PCV-3402, and it lives on the receiver side. Keep the two ideas separate: hot gas at the users trims evaporating pressure; the PCV pair keeps the receiver pressurised so liquid keeps flowing in cold weather. Say that last part as the reading of the drawing, not as a quotation &#8212; the routing is traced on PR38 but no manual text describes what either PCV is for. And note PCV-3401 carries no solenoid on PR38: PEV-3401 exists only as a register record.", "One documentation oddity worth naming: the connector text on PR22 says the hot gas comes FROM OIL SEPARATOR V-341, while PR38 and the manual both source it from the compressor discharge header. Same fluid, different description."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 11/12 &#183; hot gas <span class="sub">&#8212; <b>4&quot;-AN-340008-3L1</b> off the discharge header &#183; PR38 &#8594; PR08 / PR22</span></div><h2>Hot gas and head-pressure control</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>The master PFD &#8212; the hot-gas take-off and the receiver-pressure pair</span><span class="you">&#9632; hot gas &#183; PCV-3401 / PCV-3402</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="cycle" data-hi="HOTGAS PCV-3402"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><ul class="points"><li><b>Tapped off 8&quot;-AN-340004-3C1</b> at a spec break 3C1&#8594;3L1, into <b>4&quot;-AN-340008-3L1</b>, then <b>1&#189;&quot;-AN-000208-3L1</b> to E-311 and <b>3&quot;-AN-000125-3L1</b> to E-354.</li><li><b>Both lines inject into the liquid feed</b> just upstream of the evaporator, not into a defrost header: <b>PCV-3111 (4 barg)</b> at E-311, <b>PCV-3540 (2.8 barg)</b> at E-354.</li><li><b>Head pressure is the other pair:</b> PCV-3401 at 25.45 barg in the discharge header and PCV-3402 at 24.7 barg feeding the receiver vapour space.</li></ul></div><div><div class="hcard eqc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9888; Two mechanisms, do not mix them</span><span>hot gas vs head pressure</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">At the users</span><span class="v">trims evaporating pressure / prevents over-cooling</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">At the receiver</span><span class="v">keeps liquid moving when the air is cold &#8212; <b>a reading of PR38, not a manual statement</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Pilots</span><span class="v">PEV-3111 &#183; PEV-3540 &#183; PEV-3402 &#183; <b>PEV-3401 register only, not on PR38</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Register</span><span class="v">no set pressure stored for PCV-3401 or PCV-3402</span></div></div></div></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b>PR22 labels the hot-gas source <b>FROM OIL SEPARATOR V-341</b>; PR38 and the manual take the same line off the <b>compressor discharge header</b>. Functionally identical, differently described.</div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="2" data-block="Block 2" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Close the walkthrough with the protection of last resort.", "Everything in this unit is designed to 28 barg and everything relieves at 28 barg: the receiver pair on a change-over valve with a lock device, the blocked-outlet valve on each compressor, and the pair on each oil separator. The only exception is the oil-pump relief, which is 6 bar differential, not absolute.", "All of it goes to the ammonia flare header on drawing PRO13-ING-PR43, and remember slide 08: flaring ammonia is an emergency measure, not a routine one.", "The statement to write down: Unit 340 has NO shutdown valve and NO blowdown valve. It is protected by tripping the machines and by the relief valves. There is nothing to slam shut, which changes how you think about an emergency here.", "Charging enters at 354-HV-201, a one-and-a-half inch double-block connection teed into the E-354 liquid line on PR38, under the ammonia charging procedure &#8212; and remember the oil charging point is a separate connection on the oil separator, slide 27."]'><div class="kicker">Block 2 &#183; 12/12 &#183; relief and utilities <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual Table 7.6 &#183; PR38 &#183; PR34&#8211;PR37 &#183; flare header PR43</span></div><h2>Relief, vent, drain and charging</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>Relief devices, their routes, and the vent / drain / charging connections</span><span class="you">&#9632; PSV-3401A/B &#183; package reliefs &#183; charging</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="relief"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><table class="data" style="font-size:10.6px;"><tr><th>Device</th><th>Protects</th><th>Set</th></tr><tr><td><span class="k">PSV-3401A/B</span></td><td>V-343 &#183; on COV-3401, lock device, fire case</td><td><b>28 barg</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">PSV-3411.1&#8230;4</span></td><td>C-341-x &#183; blocked outlet &#8212; a screw compressor never stops pumping</td><td><b>28 barg</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">PSV-3417.xA / xB</span></td><td>V-341-x &#183; change-over, dry fire</td><td><b>28 barg</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">PSV-3418.1&#8230;4</span></td><td>P-341-x oil pump &#183; back to pump suction</td><td><b>6 bar diff.</b></td></tr></table></div><div><ul class="points"><li><b>Route:</b> 1&#189;&quot;-FL-340002-3L1 &#8594; 3&quot;-FL-340003-1C1 &#8594; <b>3&quot;-FL-000026-1C1</b> &#8594; ammonia flare header (PRO13-ING-PR43); a 3&quot; branch off the V-343 inlet downcomer runs there too.</li><li><b>340-CK-081</b> (6&quot;) is the check valve in the condensate line <b>from the condensers into V-343</b> &#8212; it stops the receiver blowing back into the banks.</li><li><b>Drains and vents:</b> 340-HV-008 / -010 / -012 / -017 / -030 &#183; receiver drain <b>340-HV-026</b> (2&quot;).</li><li><b>Charging point 354-HV-201</b> (1&#189;&quot;, two valves in series, on PR38) &#8212; follow <b>QD23-HSEQ-FR03A</b>, the ammonia charging procedure.</li><li><b>No SDV and no BDV in Unit 340.</b> Protection is by tripping the machines and by the relief valves.</li></ul></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b>Manual Table 7.6 lists PSV-3418.4 as protecting <b>P-441-4</b> &#8212; a typo for <b>P-341-4</b>. It is still live in the database, in both the service and the equipment field.</div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="3" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Four identical packages, three running and one on standby. The UFD labels them four times thirty-three per cent, which is the same statement: three machines make ninety-nine per cent of the duty.", "The sentence to underline is from Manual section 7.4 and from the vendor manual alike: the number of compressors in service is selected MANUALLY by the operator, and the packages are started from the panel. There is no automatic sequencing logic.", "Each package is a complete little plant: compressor, motor, oil separator, oil cooler with ten fans, oil pump, duplex filters and an oil heater, with its own junction box and local panel.", "New from the vendor equipment list, and in no P&amp;ID and no database table: UCP-K49-JM101, the unit control panel with its own HMI, and JB-K49-JM11, the junction box installed on the chiller skid and wired back to that panel. When somebody says the package panel, that is its tag.", "The packages are twins on the drawings &#8212; every tag, size and set point checked identical across PR34 to PR37 apart from the suffix. In the database only train 1 is digitised at equipment level, so the other three are inferred."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 1/13 &#183; the compressor trains <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual Table 7.3 &#183; P&amp;IDs PR34&#8211;PR37 &#183; vendor manual slides 4 / 6</span></div><h2>PK-341-1&#8230;4 &#8212; four trains, 3 working + 1 standby</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>Where they sit in the cycle</span><span class="you">&#9632; PK-341-1&#8230;4</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="cycle" data-hi="PK-341"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><div class="collbl">What is inside one package</div><ul class="points"><li><b>C-341-x</b> oil-flooded screw compressor + <b>MC-341-x</b> motor, 560 kW, 690 V, VFD.</li><li><b>V-341-x</b> oil separator (also the oil reservoir) &#183; <b>X-341-x</b> oil heater inside it.</li><li><b>E-341-x</b> air-cooled oil cooler, <b>10 fans</b> &#183; <b>P-341-x</b> oil pump &#183; <b>F-341-xA/B</b> duplex filters, 1 duty + 1 standby.</li><li>Junction box <b>P-PK-PK-341-x</b> (Siemens ET200SP, Zone 1/21) and a local panel per package.</li><li><b>Vendor control system:</b> <b>UCP-K49-JM101</b> unit control panel <b>with HMI</b>, and <b>JB-K49-JM11</b>, the junction box on the chiller skid wired back to it.</li></ul></div><div><div class="hcard eqc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9888; How they are run</span><span>manual selection</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">Configuration</span><span class="v">3 working + 1 standby &#183; UFD and vendor both say 4 &#215; 33 %</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Start / stop</span><span class="v">operator choice, <b>manually from the panel</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Capacity</span><span class="v">slide valve, on PIC-3552 in cascade</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Trips</span><span class="v">each package trips itself &#8212; no cross-package effect</span></div></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b>Compressor model reads <b>VR-V30T-28</b> on the P&amp;ID data block and in the vendor manual, but <b>VR-V307-28</b> in the equipment register. <b>UCP-K49-JM101</b> and <b>JB-K49-JM11</b> appear in the vendor equipment list only &#8212; no P&amp;ID, no database row.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="3" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["The machine itself, now with the vendor data block behind it: Grasso Germany, model VR-V30T-28, an oil-flooded screw compressor running at 2 950 rpm for 560 kW of cooling duty.", "Read the four process numbers together, because they are the machine in one line: suction 3.62 barg at 1.25 degrees, discharge 25.43 barg at 88 degrees, and the oil arriving at the compressor at 29.53 barg and 90 degrees.", "Notice that the oil arrives ABOVE the discharge pressure &#8212; 29.53 against 25.43 barg. That is the whole reason there is a pump: oil has to be injected against the compression pressure, and the total oil flow is 135 litres per minute, the full output of P-341-x.", "Oil-flooded means the oil is inside the compression space, not just in the bearings. It seals the gap between the rotors, carries away the heat of compression and lubricates. That is why the next four slides are about oil.", "Four times thirty-three per cent, three working plus one standby. Four trips protect each machine: low-low suction 1.5 bar absolute, high-high discharge 27.5 barg, high-high discharge temperature 110 degrees, and low-low oil differential."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 2/13 &#183; the machine <span class="sub">&#8212; C-341-x &#183; <b>Grasso Germany VR-V30T-28</b> &#183; vendor manual slide 33 &#183; PR34&#8211;PR37 sheet 1</span></div><h2>C-341-x &#8212; the screw compressor</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>Gas path through one package &#8212; suction, compressor, oil separator, discharge</span><span class="you">&#9632; C-341-x &#183; the four trips</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="comp"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><table class="data" style="font-size:10.2px;"><tr><th>Vendor data block</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Manufacturer &#183; type</td><td><b>Grasso Germany</b> &#183; oil-flooded screw</td></tr><tr><td>Model &#183; speed</td><td><b>VR-V30T-28</b> &#183; <b>2 950 rpm</b></td></tr><tr><td>Cooling duty</td><td><b>560 kW</b></td></tr><tr><td>Suction</td><td><b>3.62 barg</b> @ <b>1.25 &#176;C</b></td></tr><tr><td>Discharge</td><td><b>25.43 barg</b> @ <b>88 &#176;C</b></td></tr><tr><td>Oil inlet to compressor</td><td><b>29.53 barg</b> @ <b>90 &#176;C</b></td></tr><tr><td>Total oil flow</td><td><b>135 LPM</b></td></tr><tr><td>Packages</td><td><b>4 &#215; 33 %</b> &#183; 3 working + 1 standby</td></tr></table></div><div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-screw-compressor-3d.png" alt="PK-341 screw compressor package, vendor 3D view" style="max-height:132px;"><div class="cap">Vendor manual &#8212; the compressor on the skid</div></div><div class="hcard eqc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9888; The four trips of this machine</span><span>all LSD</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">PALL-3411.x</span><span class="v">suction low-low &#183; <b>1.5 bar abs</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PAHH-3412.x</span><span class="v">discharge high-high &#183; <b>27.5 barg</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">TAHH-3412.x</span><span class="v">discharge temp. high-high &#183; <b>110 &#176;C</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">PDALL-3414.x</span><span class="v">oil differential low-low &#183; <b>1 bar</b></span></div></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="3" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["One slide of principle, because everything that goes wrong with this machine makes sense once you can picture the rotors.", "Two helical rotors mesh inside a casing. Gas is drawn into the space between two lobes at the suction end; as the rotors turn, that space is closed off from suction, becomes a sealed pocket, and is squeezed smaller and smaller as it travels along the rotors until it opens to the discharge port.", "Oil is injected into that space for three reasons at once: it SEALS the clearance between rotor and rotor and between rotor and casing, it COOLS the gas as it is compressed, and it LUBRICATES the bearings and the meshing surfaces. That is what oil-flooded means, and it is why oil and gas leave the machine together and have to be separated afterwards.", "Now the consequence that matters to an operator: this is a POSITIVE-DISPLACEMENT machine. It has no surge line and no stall. If you close the outlet it does not stop pumping, it keeps building pressure until something gives. That is exactly why PSV-3411.x exists on every compressor, set at 28 barg, blocked-outlet case.", "Second consequence: there is no throttling of a screw compressor by valve. Capacity is changed by changing the effective length of the compression, which is the slide valve on the next slide."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 3/13 &#183; principle <span class="sub">&#8212; vendor manual slide 36 &#183; screw compressor design</span></div><h2>How a screw compressor works</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1.05fr 1fr;"><ul class="points"><li><b>Two meshing helical rotors</b> in a close-clearance casing. Gas enters the space between the lobes at the suction end.</li><li><b>The pocket seals.</b> As the rotors turn, that volume is cut off from suction, then squeezed smaller as it travels axially, until it uncovers the discharge port.</li><li><b>Oil is injected into the compression space</b> &#8212; it <b>seals</b> the rotor clearances, <b>cools</b> the gas and <b>lubricates</b> the bearings. Oil and ammonia therefore leave together and must be separated in <b>V-341-x</b>.</li><li><b>Positive displacement.</b> There is no surge and no stall &#8212; a screw compressor will keep pumping into a closed outlet until something fails. <b>That is why PSV-3411.x, 28 barg, blocked-outlet case, sits on every machine.</b></li><li><b>So capacity cannot be throttled with a valve</b> &#8212; it is changed by the slide valve, next slide.</li></ul><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-screw-design.png" alt="Screw compressor design, vendor manual slide 36" style="max-height:216px;"><div class="cap">Vendor manual &#8212; screw compressor design</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="4" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["This is how a screw compressor is turned down, and it is worth understanding physically rather than as a percentage on a screen.", "The slide valve is a machined block that moves PARALLEL to the rotor axis along the bottom of the rotor casing. As it moves it changes the area of the opening into the casing: it shortens or lengthens the region over which compression actually happens, and it lets the gas that is not being compressed return to the suction side.", "It is moved HYDRAULICALLY, and the hydraulic fluid is the compressor lube oil itself &#8212; which is another reason the oil pump must be running and at pressure before the machine can do anything. Four solenoids ZEV-3411 to ZEV-3414 drive it; on the HMI faceplate for SV-341-1 they are labelled Y1, Y4, Y2 and Y3.", "Position comes back on ZT-3411.x as zero to one hundred per cent, and the PLC drives the valve from PT-3552, the common suction pressure. The compressor packages themselves are started MANUALLY from the panel; only the capacity is automatic.", "Now the open point, and do not let anyone leave with one number: the vendor data block says the slide valve range is 20 to 100 per cent, manual section 7.7.1 says 100 per cent down to 15 to 30 per cent, and the start-up sequence unloads it below 5 per cent and then holds it at about 15 per cent. Three different figures, all in approved documents."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 4/13 &#183; capacity control <span class="sub">&#8212; vendor manual slides 34 / 35 &#183; ZT-3411.x &#183; ZEV-3411&#8230;3414 &#183; PT-3552</span></div><h2>Capacity control &#8212; the slide valve</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1.15fr 1fr;"><ul class="points"><li><b>What it is:</b> a block that moves <b>parallel to the rotor axis</b> and changes the area of the opening in the bottom of the rotor casing &#8212; it shortens the region of compression and <b>returns the surplus gas to suction</b>.</li><li><b>How it moves:</b> <b>hydraulically, on the compressor lube oil</b>. No oil pressure, no capacity control &#8212; and no start.</li><li><b>Solenoids:</b> <b>ZEV-3411 / 3412 / 3413 / 3414</b> (Danfoss BZ240C, 230 VAC). The HMI shows them on the <b>SV-341-1</b> faceplate as <b>Y1 &#183; Y4 &#183; Y2 &#183; Y3</b>.</li><li><b>Feedback:</b> <b>ZT-3411.x, 0&#8211;100 %</b>, to the PLC.</li><li><b>Who decides:</b> the PLC drives the slide valve from <b>PT-3552</b>, the common suction pressure. <b>The packages themselves are started manually from the panel.</b></li></ul><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-slidevalve-sensor.png" alt="Slide valve and its position sensor, vendor manual" style="max-height:186px;"><div class="cap">Vendor manual &#8212; slide valve and position sensor</div></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b><b>A-22 &#183; slide-valve range, three figures.</b> Vendor data block: <b>20&#8211;100 %</b>. Manual &#167;7.7.1: 100 % down to <b>15&#8211;30 %</b>. Start-up sequence: unload to <b>&lt; 5 %</b>, then hold <b>&#8776; 15 %</b>. Likely a control range, a mechanical unload position and a start hold &#8212; but no source says so. Not resolved here.</div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="3" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["The oil separator is the second half of the compressor. Oil and gas leave the machine together; this vessel takes the oil out and stores it, and only clean vapour goes on to the discharge header.", "So it is a vessel with two jobs: separator and reservoir. That is why the oil heater X-341-x sits inside it, and why the oil level switch LSL-3417.x is a protection rather than a convenience.", "The vendor gives the operating levels measured from the BOTTOM of the vessel, and these are the numbers the database does not hold: high 350 millimetres, normal 220 millimetres, and the low-level TRIP at 110 millimetres. That 110 is the LSL-3417.x set point the register carries as null.", "Ninety per cent of the oil is knocked out here by simple physics &#8212; the mixture splashes against the wall and gravity does the rest. The remaining ten per cent is the coalescers job, which is the next slide.", "The relief is a pair on a change-over valve, both set at 28 barg and both annotated dry fire &#8212; the same design pressure as the whole loop."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 5/13 &#183; the oil separator <span class="sub">&#8212; V-341-x &#183; FV/28 barg &#183; PR34&#8211;PR37 sheet 2 &#183; vendor manual slides 25 / 26</span></div><h2>V-341-x &#8212; the oil separator</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr;"><ul class="points"><li><b>Design FV / 28 barg</b>, &#8722;15/120 &#176;C, TL&#8211;TL <b>4 600 mm</b>, internal volume <b>2.12 m&#179;</b>, internal elements <b>coalescers</b>.</li><li><b>Two jobs:</b> it separates the oil from the ammonia AND it is <b>the oil reservoir of the package</b> &#8212; the whole oil charge lives here.</li><li><b>90 % of the oil drops out here</b> by splashing against the wall and by gravity, before the gas ever reaches the coalescer section.</li><li><b>PSV-3417.xA / xB &#183; 28 barg</b> on a change-over arrangement, both marked <b>dry fire</b>, to the package flare line. Gas out through <b>341-CK-112.x</b> to the 4&quot; discharge line.</li></ul><div class="hcard ioc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9636; Operating levels &#183; from the BOTTOM of the vessel</span><span>vendor</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">High level</span><span class="v"><b>350 mm</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Normal level</span><span class="v"><b>220 mm</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Low level &#8212; TRIP</span><span class="v"><b>110 mm</b> &#8594; LSL-3417.x</span></div></div></div><div class="hcard ioc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9636; What is measured on it</span><span>V-341-x</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">Pressure</span><span class="v">PT-3412.x &#8594; PAH 27 / PAHH 27.5 barg</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Oil temperature</span><span class="v">TT-3417.x &#8594; <b>TIC-3417.1</b> &#183; X-341-x heater</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Oil level</span><span class="v">LSL-3417.x &#8594; LAL-3417.x &#183; SG-3417A/B.x</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Separator &#916;P</span><span class="v">PDG-3415.x &#183; local gauge, 0..5 bar</span></div></div></div></div><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.1fr;margin-top:6px;"><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-oilseparator-ga.png" alt="Oil separator general arrangement with operating levels, vendor manual" style="max-height:96px;"><div class="cap">Vendor manual &#8212; V-341-x GA and levels</div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b>Vessel diameter: <b>762 mm OD</b> on the P&amp;ID data block, <b>700 mm ID</b> in the equipment register. Both are recorded; neither is corrected here. The <b>110 mm low-level trip</b> is the vendor figure for the LSL-3417.x set point the database holds as <b>null</b>.</div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="3" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["The last ten per cent of the oil does not fall out by gravity, so the separator carries FOUR coalescer elements, reached through the manway. They catch the fine mist the vessel cannot drop.", "What happens to the oil they catch is the part people get wrong: it is returned to the compressor SUCTION through the oil return lines. Those lines carry a sight glass and needle valves, and the vendor is explicit that the needle valve setting is a ONE-TIME adjustment made by the commissioning engineer.", "Say the warning as the vendor says it: mis-operating those valves loses performance and increases oil carry-over. If you find a needle valve on an oil return line, do not touch it &#8212; check the sight glass and report what you see.", "A small amount of oil always gets past and reaches the chiller with the refrigerant. That oil has to be drained manually &#8212; it is a scheduled task, not a fault.", "Maintenance rule, and it is absolute: the compressor must be shut down and the vessel pumped down before the manway is opened. Then any clogged element is replaced and every element is retightened so the seal is tight, because a loose coalescer shows up as high oil carry-over."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 6/13 &#183; separator internals <span class="sub">&#8212; vendor manual slides 26 / 27 &#183; 4 coalescer elements &#183; PDG-3415.x</span></div><h2>Oil separator internals &#8212; coalescers and the oil return</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1.25fr 1fr;"><ul class="points"><li><b>4 coalescer elements</b>, maintained through the <b>manway</b>. They catch the oil mist the vessel body cannot drop out.</li><li><b>The captured oil goes back to the compressor SUCTION</b> through the oil return lines &#8212; not to the reservoir.</li><li><b>Those lines carry a sight glass and needle valves.</b> The setting is a <b>one-time commissioning adjustment</b>. <b>Mis-operating them loses performance and increases oil carry-over.</b> Check the glass, do not turn the valve.</li><li><b>A small amount of oil always reaches the chiller</b> with the refrigerant and <b>must be drained manually</b>.</li><li><b>Before opening:</b> the compressor must be <b>shut down</b> and the vessel <b>pumped down</b>. Replace any clogged element, then <b>retighten every element</b> so the seal is tight.</li></ul><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-coalescer.png" alt="Coalescer elements inside the oil separator, vendor manual" style="max-height:198px;"><div class="cap">Vendor manual &#8212; coalescer elements</div></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b><b>A-23 &#183; coalescer change &#916;P, three figures in one vendor document.</b> Slide 27 says replace above <b>0.8 barg</b> across PDG-3415.x; the monitoring schedule row 23 says <b>3 psid</b> (&#8776; 0.21 bar); manual &#167;7.8 says the differential should be <b>not less than 0.25 psid</b>. No number is adopted here &#8212; ask the OEM before acting on one.</div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="4" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["This is the slide to spend time on, because most compressor trips in a screw plant are oil trips. The vendor puts it bluntly: lubrication is a critical loop for oil-flooded screw compressors and a failure in it will result in compressor damage.", "The oil does three jobs at once: it lubricates the bearings, it removes the heat of compression, and it SEALS the gap between the rotors. Lose the oil and you do not lose lubrication only &#8212; you lose the compressor as a compressor.", "There are two circuits and they share one cooler. Circuit one is the INJECTION oil: it runs on differential pressure straight from the separator through the cooler to the injection ports. Circuit two is the FUNCTIONAL oil: separator, pump, cooler, duplex filters, then the 2 inch oil header that feeds the bearings and the slide-valve hydraulics.", "The vendor closes the loop on how one cooler serves two circuits: of its ten fans, FIVE cool the injection oil and FIVE cool the functional oil. That is the physical explanation of the two-circuit drawing, and it appears in no other source.", "The protections are oil differential pressure, oil temperature and oil level, and they are the subject of the next slide."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 7/13 &#183; the lube-oil system <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual &#167;7.7.7 &#183; Fig. 7.11 &#183; PR34&#8211;PR37 sheet 2 &#183; vendor slides 17 / 19 / 28 / 29</span></div><h2>The lube-oil system &#8212; two circuits, one cooler</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:5px 8px 2px;"><div class="lbl"><span>One oil system, two circuits through one cooler &#8212; injection and functional</span><span class="you">&#9632; oil &#183; pump &#183; filters &#183; protections</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="oil"></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><ul class="points"><li><b>Circuit 1 &#8212; INJECTION oil, unpumped:</b> V-341-x nozzle N5 &#8594; cooler circuit 1 &#8594; <b>341-HR-120.x / 121.x</b> &#8594; compressor nozzles <b>B</b> and <b>B1</b>.</li><li><b>Circuit 2 &#8212; FUNCTIONAL oil, pumped:</b> nozzle N3 &#8594; strainer &#8594; <b>P-341-x</b> &#8594; cooler circuit 2 &#8594; <b>F-341-xA/B</b> &#8594; <b>2&quot; oil header</b> &#8594; bearings (A, D) and the slide-valve cylinder (C).</li><li><b>PDCV-3417.x</b> self-acting differential regulator returns surplus pump flow to the separator &#8212; that is what sets oil header pressure.</li><li><b>One cooler, two circuits:</b> Note 1 on PR34 sheet 2, and the vendor adds the physical split &#8212; of the <b>10 fans, 5 cool the injection oil and 5 cool the functional oil</b>.</li></ul></div><div><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;margin-top:0;"><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-lubeoil-schematic.png" alt="Lube-oil system schematic, vendor manual" style="max-height:112px;"><div class="cap">Vendor &#8212; lube-oil system</div></div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-oil-two-loops.png" alt="Injection oil and functional oil loops, vendor manual" style="max-height:112px;"><div class="cap">Vendor &#8212; injection vs functional oil</div></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b><b>A-24 &#183; the 5 + 5 fan split</b> is a vendor-only statement. No P&amp;ID, manual table or database record divides the ten fans of E-341-x between the two circuits.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="2" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["A small machine that the whole package depends on. It must be running and at pressure before the compressor can start, and it keeps running through coast-down.", "It is a positive-displacement GEAR pump, one times one hundred per cent &#8212; no spare. The vendor data block: 135 litres per minute at 1 450 rpm, motor MP-341-x at 4 kilowatts, model BG-KF 100 LG 25 slash 346-D15-GJS-ATEX.", "Two things protect it. A suction strainer, 341-ST-103.x, keeps dust particles out of the gears. And PSV-3418.x, set at 6 bar DIFFERENTIAL, relieves back to the pump suction rather than to a header &#8212; it protects the pump from its own over-pressure, it is not a system relief.", "Downstream, check valve 341-CK-111.x keeps the header primed when the pump stops.", "The operating rule from the vendor, and it is the same sentence as on the instrumentation slide: if the oil differential pressure falls below 1.5 bar D, the automatic sequence halts and the compressor is tripped."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 8/13 &#183; the oil pump <span class="sub">&#8212; P-341-x &#183; vendor manual slides 20 / 23 &#183; Manual &#167;7.7.8</span></div><h2>P-341-x &#8212; the oil pump</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);margin-top:6px;"><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-oilpump-pid.png" alt="Oil pump on the vendor P&amp;ID extract" style="max-height:92px;"><div class="cap">P&amp;ID extract</div></div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-oilpump-gears.png" alt="Oil pump internal gears, vendor manual" style="max-height:92px;"><div class="cap">the gears</div></div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-oilpump-psv.png" alt="Oil pump pressure safety valve, vendor manual" style="max-height:92px;"><div class="cap">PSV-3418.x</div></div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-oilpump-psv-setting.png" alt="Oil pump PSV setting, vendor manual" style="max-height:92px;"><div class="cap">6 bar differential</div></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><ul class="points"><li><b>Positive-displacement gear pump, 1 &#215; 100 %</b> &#183; <b>135 LPM</b> &#183; <b>1 450 rpm</b> &#183; casing 34 barg.</li><li><b>Model BG-KF 100 LG 25 / 346-D15-GJS-ATEX</b> &#183; motor <b>MP-341-x, 4 kW</b>, 690 V, DOL.</li><li><b>Suction strainer 341-ST-103.x</b> protects the gears from dust particles; <b>341-CK-111.x</b> on the discharge keeps the header primed.</li><li><b>PSV-3418.x &#183; set 6 bar DIFFERENTIAL</b>, relieving <b>back to the pump suction</b> &#8212; a pump protection, not a system relief.</li></ul></div><div><div class="hcard eqc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9888; The rule the pump exists for</span><span>vendor</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">Oil &#916;P to start</span><span class="v"><b>&#8805; 1.5 bar D</b> &#183; below it the start is inhibited</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Oil &#916;P while running</span><span class="v"><b>below 1.5 bar D the sequence halts and the compressor trips</b></span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Post-lubrication</span><span class="v">the pump runs on <b>&#8776; 30 s</b> after the motor stops</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Spare philosophy</span><span class="v"><b>1 &#215; 100 %</b> &#8212; there is no standby oil pump</span></div></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b>Manual Table 7.4 prints the oil pump at <b>5.30 kW</b> and the heater at <b>4.00 kW</b>; the rows are misaligned in the PDF. The database and the vendor data block both say oil pump <b>4 kW</b>, so the deck prints 4 kW.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="3" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["The pump has no shaft seal, and that is deliberate. Torque crosses a sealed containment shell between an outer rotor mounted on the motor shaft and an inner rotor mounted directly on the pump shaft, and the magnetic force between them turns the pump.", "The vendor puts it exactly this way: the magnetic coupling serves as a shaft seal and for transmitting the torque. So there is no dynamic seal and no leak path for ammonia-laden oil &#8212; that is why it was chosen for this service.", "Now the failure mode, and it is quiet. A magnetic coupling has a maximum torque, and on overload it DECOUPLES. The motor keeps turning, the pump does not pump, and oil circulation is lost with no noise and no obvious sign. The oil differential is what catches it &#8212; which is another reason PDI-3414.1 matters.", "Read the three hazard levels out loud, they are the OEM wording. DANGER, strong magnetic field: risk to life for people with active implanted cardiac devices &#8212; keep at least 2 metres from unmounted components and at least 0.5 metres from installed couplings and the bell housing. WARNING: uncontrolled mutual attraction between magnetic or magnetisable parts within 0.5 metres. ATTENTION: magnetic media such as discs and cards are damaged within 1 metre.", "And the vendor line that belongs on every job card: exercise extreme caution if the motor is removed for maintenance. An unmounted magnet assembly is far more dangerous than an installed one."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 9/13 &#183; the magnetic coupling <span class="sub">&#8212; vendor manual slides 21 / 22 &#183; Manual Fig. 7.12 &#183; OEM hazard notices</span></div><h2>The magnetic coupling &#8212; and its hazards</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);margin-top:6px;"><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-magcoupling-cutaway.png" alt="Magnetic coupling cutaway, vendor manual" style="max-height:108px;"><div class="cap">outer rotor &#183; shell &#183; inner rotor</div></div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-magcoupling-warning.png" alt="Magnetic coupling warning notice, vendor manual" style="max-height:108px;"><div class="cap">OEM warning notice</div></div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-magcoupling-danger.png" alt="Magnetic coupling danger notice, vendor manual" style="max-height:108px;"><div class="cap">OEM danger notice</div></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><ul class="points"><li><b>How it works:</b> an <b>outer rotor on the motor shaft</b> and an <b>inner rotor on the pump shaft</b>, separated by a <b>containment shell</b>. Torque crosses magnetically.</li><li><b>It IS the shaft seal.</b> No dynamic seal, no leak path &#8212; the reason it is used on ammonia-laden oil.</li><li><b>It can decouple.</b> Above the maximum torque the magnets slip: the pump turns without pumping and oil circulation is lost <b>silently</b>. <b>Dry running is forbidden.</b></li><li><b>Exercise extreme caution if the motor is removed for maintenance</b> &#8212; the OEM distances below are for <b>unmounted</b> parts.</li></ul></div><div><div class="cr340 dgr"><b>danger</b><b>Strong magnetic field &#8212; risk to life</b> for people with <b>active implanted cardiac devices</b> (pacemakers, defibrillators). Keep at least <b>2 m</b> from unmounted components and at least <b>0.5 m</b> from installed couplings and the bell housing.</div><div class="cr340"><b>warning</b>Uncontrolled <b>mutual attraction</b> between magnetic or magnetisable parts within <b>0.5 m</b> &#8212; crushing injury to hands and fingers.</div><div class="cr340"><b>attention</b><b>Magnetic media</b> &#8212; discs, cards, badges &#8212; are <b>damaged within 1 m</b>.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide eqd340" data-min="2" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["One air-cooled cooler per package, and it is a serious machine in its own right: 338 kilowatts of design duty, more than half the compressor cooling duty, because almost all the heat of compression leaves the machine in the oil rather than in the gas.", "Ten fans, F-341-x-1 to 10, at 1 450 rpm, each rated 27 634 cubic metres an hour and each absorbing 3 kilowatts. Five of them cool the injection oil and five cool the functional oil &#8212; that is the vendor statement that explains how one cooler serves two circuits.", "The design conditions: 28 barg on one side and 34 barg on the other, minus 15 to 120 degrees, AISI 304 tubes with aluminium fins, and a surface area of 4 304 square metres.", "What you actually control with it is one band: oil temperature held between 40 and 60 degrees. Too cold and the oil is thick and the start is delayed; too hot and TT-3418.x trips the machine.", "The standing conflict on this unit applies to these fans as well: the drawing and the database say 400 volts, Manual Table 7.5 says 690 volts. The deck prints 400 and the change request stays open."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 10/13 &#183; the oil cooler <span class="sub">&#8212; E-341-x &#183; vendor manual slide 28 and its data block &#183; PR34&#8211;PR37 sheet 2</span></div><h2>E-341-x &#8212; the oil cooler</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;"><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-oilcooler-fans.png" alt="Oil cooler fan bank, vendor manual" style="max-height:120px;"><div class="cap">Vendor &#8212; the ten-fan bank</div></div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-oilcooler-data.png" alt="Oil cooler data block, vendor manual" style="max-height:120px;"><div class="cap">Vendor &#8212; E-341-x data block</div></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><table class="data" style="font-size:10.2px;"><tr><th>Vendor data block</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Design duty</td><td><b>338 kW</b></td></tr><tr><td>Design pressure</td><td><b>28 / 34 barg</b></td></tr><tr><td>Design temperature</td><td><b>&#8722;15 / 120 &#176;C</b></td></tr><tr><td>Materials</td><td><b>AISI 304</b> tube &#183; <b>Al</b> fin</td></tr><tr><td>Surface area</td><td><b>4 304 m&#178;</b></td></tr><tr><td>Fans</td><td><b>10</b> &#183; <b>3 kW</b> absorbed each</td></tr></table></div><div><ul class="points"><li><b>Fans F-341-x-1&#8230;10</b> &#183; <b>1 450 rpm</b> &#183; rated <b>27 634 m&#179;/h each</b>.</li><li><b>5 fans cool the INJECTION oil, 5 cool the FUNCTIONAL oil</b> &#8212; one cooler, two circuits, physically split.</li><li><b>The controlled band is 40&#8211;60 &#176;C.</b> Too cold delays the start; too hot and <b>TT-3418.x</b> trips the compressor.</li></ul><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b>Fan-motor voltage: the P&amp;ID and the database say <b>400 V</b>; Manual Table 7.5 says <b>690 V</b>. The deck prints 400 V and <b>A-2 stays open</b>. The <b>5 + 5 split</b> (<b>A-24</b>) is a vendor-only statement.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="3" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Duplex filters, one duty and one standby, and the reason for that arrangement is uptime: the element can be changed with the machine running, because you swap onto the standby housing first.", "The change trigger is a number you read in the field: replace the element when PDG-3414.x, the differential across the filter, reaches 0.7 to 1 barg. The vendor monitoring schedule gives the same threshold as 10 psid &#8212; the same number in different units, so the two sources agree.", "Remember that PDG-3414.x is a LOCAL gauge. Nobody in the control room sees a blocking filter. If nobody walks the round, nobody knows until the oil differential trips the machine.", "The procedure, in order, and the order is the safety argument. Use the isolation valves to swap onto the standby filter. Open the drain and the vent to de-pressurise the housing BEFORE opening the manway. Put an oil pan underneath. Fit the new element. Tighten the flange bolts.", "The step people skip is the de-pressurisation, and it is the one that will hurt them &#8212; the housing is full of hot oil at oil-header pressure and that oil is saturated with ammonia."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 11/13 &#183; the oil filters <span class="sub">&#8212; F-341-xA/B &#183; vendor manual slide 24 &#183; PDG-3414.x</span></div><h2>F-341-xA/B &#8212; the oil filters and how to change them</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1fr;"><div class="collbl">When to change &#8212; and why it is a field check</div><ul class="points"><li><b>Duplex: 1 duty + 1 standby.</b> The element is changed <b>with the machine running</b>, because you swap onto the standby housing first.</li><li><b>Change at PDG-3414.x = 0.7 &#8211; 1 barg.</b> The vendor monitoring schedule says <b>10 psid</b> &#8212; the same number, different units. The two sources agree.</li><li><b>PDG-3414.x is a LOCAL gauge.</b> A blocking filter is invisible from the control room until the oil &#916;P trips the machine.</li></ul><div class="collbl" style="margin-top:5px;">The changeover &#8212; in this order</div><div class="flow"><div class="step"><div class="u">1 &#183; SWAP</div><div class="t">Use the <b>isolation valves</b> to change over to the standby filter.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">2 &#183; DE-PRESSURISE</div><div class="t">Open the <b>drain and the vent</b> to de-pressurise the housing <b>BEFORE</b> opening the manway.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">3 &#183; CATCH</div><div class="t">Place an <b>oil pan</b> under the filter to collect the oil.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">4 &#183; FIT</div><div class="t">Install the <b>new element</b>.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">5 &#183; CLOSE</div><div class="t"><b>Tighten the flange bolts</b> and return the housing to standby.</div></div></div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-oilfilters-3d.png" alt="Duplex oil filters, vendor manual" style="max-height:206px;"><div class="cap">Vendor manual &#8212; F-341-xA/B</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="3" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["This is the slide that corrects something the module used to get wrong, so read it carefully.", "There is no differential-pressure TRANSMITTER on the oil system. PDI-3414.1 is a CALCULATION: the PLC subtracts the oil separator pressure PT-3412.1 from the oil header pressure PT-3413.1, and the result is displayed on the HMI in bar D. The vendor states it in one sentence: this value has to be ALWAYS above 1.5 bar D, to ensure oil flow into the compressor.", "So the trip that protects every compressor IS traceable end to end &#8212; through two registered transmitters that both have I/O channels. The register has no row for PDI-3414.1 for the simple reason that a calculated tag is not a device. That is not a gap, it is how a PLC works.", "The other three protections: TT-3418.1 on the oil filter outlet trips the compressor if oil temperature goes above set point; TT-3417.1 in the separator drives TIC-3417.1, the oil temperature controller, set 50 and 70 degrees and shown ENABLED on the HMI; and LSL-3417.x is the low oil level, tripping at 110 millimetres from the bottom.", "And the two local gauges: PDG-3414.x across the filters and PDG-3415.x across the separator. Neither is on the screen, so a blocking filter or a choked coalescer is found on the round and nowhere else.", "TIC-3417.1 is worth naming out loud: it is a live controller on the compressor HMI and it appears in no database table at all. The controller is real; the registry is incomplete."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 12/13 &#183; oil instrumentation <span class="sub">&#8212; vendor manual slide 18 &#183; HMI Fig. 7.3 &#183; <b>PDI-3414.1 is COMPUTED</b></span></div><h2>Lube-oil instrumentation &#8212; the &#916;P that starts and trips</h2><div class="grid2"><div><table class="data" style="font-size:10.2px;line-height:1.2;"><tr><th>Tag</th><th>Service</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td><span class="k">PT-3413.1</span></td><td>Lube oil header</td><td>pressure transmitter &#183; 0..34.47 barg</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">PT-3412.1</span></td><td>Oil separator</td><td>pressure transmitter &#183; 0..34.47 barg</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k" style="color:var(--crimson);">PDI-3414.1</span></td><td><b>computed differential</b></td><td><b>PT-3413.1 &#8722; PT-3412.1</b>, in the PLC &#183; <b>must ALWAYS be &gt; 1.5 bar D</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">TT-3418.1</span></td><td>Oil filter outlet</td><td><b>trips the compressor</b> above set point &#183; TAHH 65 &#176;C</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">TT-3417.1</span></td><td>Separator oil temp.</td><td>&#8594; <b>TIC-3417.1</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">TIC-3417.1</span></td><td>Oil temperature controller</td><td><b>SP 50 / 70 &#176;C &#183; ENABLED</b> &#183; <b>HMI-sourced</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">LSL-3417.x</span></td><td>Separator low oil level</td><td>trip at <b>110 mm</b> from the bottom</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">PDG-3414.x</span></td><td>Across the filters</td><td><b>local gauge only</b> &#183; 0..5 bar</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">PDG-3415.x</span></td><td>Across the separator</td><td><b>local gauge only</b> &#183; 0..5 bar</td></tr></table></div><div><ul class="points"><li><b>Say it plainly:</b> the register has <b>no row for PDI-3414.1 because it is a calculation, not a device</b>. The two transmitters it is computed from are both registered and both wired.</li><li><b>It is on the screen.</b> The compressor HMI shows <b>PDI-3414.1 &#8230; BarD</b> with dashed signal lines drawn from <b>PT-3413.1</b> and <b>PT-3412.1</b>.</li><li><b>Below 1.5 bar D</b> the automatic sequence halts and the compressor trips &#8212; the register splits it into an alarm at 1.5 bar and a trip at 1 bar.</li><li><b>TIC-3417.1</b> is a real, enabled controller on the HMI that exists in <b>no database table</b> &#8212; the loop is real, the registry is incomplete.</li></ul><div class="cr340"><b>resolved</b><b>A-6 &#8212; RESOLVED.</b> The lube-oil differential trip is <b>fully traceable</b>: PT-3413.1 and PT-3412.1 are registered instruments and PDI-3414.1 is the PLC subtraction of the two. The deck no longer claims a missing instrument.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="3" data-block="Block 3" data-presenter="" data-notes='["The oil is a consumable with a specification, and the vendor treats it that way. Three activities: charging, changing, and analysis.", "Charging goes INTO THE OIL SEPARATOR, through a dedicated valve on the vessel. It can be done during operation with an external pump, or during a shutdown &#8212; and the vendor says the shutdown is the preferred option.", "The reason is the one warning on the charging slide: limit the ambient air entering the system, because air in the loop reduces performance. Every connection you break is a chance to admit air.", "Changing: every 16 000 operating hours, or sooner if the oil looks dirty or dark in colour, whichever comes first &#8212; and it is typical to replace the oil after the FIRST 8 000 hours following commissioning, because that first charge collects the commissioning debris. Drain through the drain valves on the oil separator.", "Analysis once a year, to decide whether the oil needs replacing rather than guessing from its colour. And two absolutes: use only the manufacturer-recommended oil from the lubricant list, and ensure there is no water in the oil when refilling."]'><div class="kicker">Block 3 &#183; 13/13 &#183; oil housekeeping <span class="sub">&#8212; vendor manual slides 30 / 31 &#183; charging &#183; change interval &#183; analysis</span></div><h2>Oil housekeeping &#8212; charging, changing, analysis</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1.3fr 1fr;"><div class="grid2" style="gap:8px 16px;"><div><div class="collbl">Charging</div><ul class="points"><li><b>Charge into the OIL SEPARATOR</b>, through the dedicated valve on the vessel.</li><li>During operation with an <b>external pump</b>, or during a <b>shutdown &#8212; the preferred option</b>.</li><li><b>Limit the ambient air entering the system</b> &#8212; air <b>reduces performance</b>.</li><li><b>Use only the manufacturer-recommended oil</b> (see the lubricant list).</li></ul></div><div><div class="collbl">Changing &amp; analysis</div><ul class="points"><li><b>Change every 16 000 operating hours</b>, or sooner if the oil <b>looks dirty or darker in colour</b> &#8212; whichever comes first.</li><li><b>Typically replace after the first 8 000 hours</b> following commissioning.</li><li><b>Oil analysis once a year</b> to decide whether it needs replacing.</li><li><b>Drain through the drain valves on the oil separator</b>, and <b>ensure no water is in the oil when refilling</b>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-oil-charging.png" alt="Oil charging point on the separator, vendor manual" style="max-height:198px;"><div class="cap">Vendor manual &#8212; the charging valve</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide inter brk" data-min="15" data-block="Break" data-presenter="" data-notes='["15-minute break. Give the exact return time out loud and write it somewhere visible.", "Block 3 next: valves, instruments, loops, the HMI screens, alarms, the cause-and-effect matrix and the interlocks, then start-up, shutdown, the monitoring schedule, the overhaul plan and troubleshooting.", "Good moment to collect the questions raised during the walkthrough so they can be answered against the drawings after the break."]'><div><div class="big">&#9749; 15:00</div><h2>Break</h2><p>15 minutes. Block 3 next: <b>control, protection, operation and maintenance</b> of Unit 340.</p></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="3" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Teach the grammar, not the list. A valve symbol is a body that says a valve is here, plus an actuator on top that says which family it belongs to and how it moves.", "Yellow is reserved for the SAFETY family. In this unit that means the relief valves only &#8212; there is no shutdown valve to paint yellow. Every PEV and ZEV solenoid is the same solenoid symbol drawn white: they are operating devices on the PCS, not trips.", "The register holds 18 relief valves, 6 control valves and 18 solenoids for this unit. The relief settings are the fastest way to know where you are: 28 barg everywhere, except 6 bar differential across an oil pump.", "Two data gaps to state, not hide. Fail action, actuator type and valve size are null on every valve record, so this deck cannot tell you a fail-safe position from the database. And ZERO manual valves are digitised for Unit 340 &#8212; do not quote a count for a valve line-up or a lock-out exercise.", "The set pressures of PCV-3401 and PCV-3402 are also missing from the register; they exist only on the drawing and the HMI."]'><div class="kicker">Protection &#183; the valve families <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual Table 7.6 &#183; PR34&#8211;PR38 &#183; instrumented valves in the register</span></div><h2>Valves &#8212; relief, control, solenoid</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:6px 10px 3px;"><div class="lbl"><span>Unit 340 &#8212; valves by family, drawn as they are built</span><span class="you">yellow = SAFETY only &#183; solenoid box = on/off &#183; diaphragm = control &#183; spring = relief</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="valves340"></div></div><div class="note"><b>18 relief &#183; 6 control &#183; 18 solenoid</b> instrumented valves in the register. <b>Zero manual valves are digitised for Unit 340</b> &#8212; isolation, drain, vent and bypass valves exist on the P&amp;IDs but not in the database, so no count is quoted here. <b>fail_action, actuator_type and valve_size are null on every record</b>, and the set pressures of PCV-3401 / PCV-3402 are absent &#8212; they live only on PR38 and the HMI. Isolation philosophy <b>SAF01-ING-FR01</b> &#183; relief and blowdown <b>SAF05-ING-FR01</b>.</div></section>
<section class="slide m101inst" data-min="4" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Where each measurement lives. 151 instrumentation assets are registered for this unit; click a family tile to print the records behind it.", "The teaching point is the outline, not the tag: a circle is a field device wired back through a junction box, a square is shared control inside the PCS with no cable, a diamond is safety logic that can shut something down.", "PDI-3414.x is drawn as a SQUARE on this sheet, and that is the correction to notice. It is not a field device at all &#8212; it is the PLC subtracting PT-3412.x from PT-3413.x, and that is why the register has no row for it.", "Several tags are on the HMI and on no drawing and in no table: SV-341-1, the slide valve itself with its Y1 to Y4 solenoid indicators; II-3411.1, the compressor motor current in amps; SD-341-1; 341-ST-101.1, the suction strainer; and PK-341-V1, which is the vendor tag for V-341-1. Know them so a screen does not surprise you.", "Two register defects to mention while the tiles are open: the temperature transmitters of train 1 read minus 50 to 200 degrees while trains 2, 3 and 4 read minus 50 to 120 for the same service, and the operating-value column is empty for every single instrument in this unit."]'><div class="kicker">Operating interface <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual Table 7.7 &#183; <b>151 instrumentation assets in the register</b></span></div><h2>Instrumentation &#8212; what you watch</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:6px 10px 3px;"><div class="lbl"><span>Unit 340 &#8212; where each measurement lives</span><span class="you">the outline is the data &#183; &#9675; field &#183; &#9632; PCS &#183; &#9671; safety logic</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="instr340"></div></div><div class="mgrid" id="itiles340" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(8,1fr);"><button class="objtile" style="--acc:#0B5CAD" data-v="P"><div class="ic">&#9684;</div><div class="n" data-cnt="P">&#183;</div><div class="l">Pressure (P)</div></button><button class="objtile" style="--acc:#7A3FB3" data-v="L"><div class="ic">&#9636;</div><div class="n" data-cnt="L">&#183;</div><div class="l">Level (L)</div></button><button class="objtile" style="--acc:#B26A00" data-v="T"><div class="ic">&#9788;</div><div class="n" data-cnt="T">&#183;</div><div class="l">Temp (T)</div></button><button class="objtile" style="--acc:#1F8A4C" data-v="F"><div class="ic">&#8674;</div><div class="n" data-cnt="F">&#183;</div><div class="l">Flow (F)</div></button><button class="objtile" style="--acc:#0E7C86" data-v="A"><div class="ic">&#9879;</div><div class="n" data-cnt="A">&#183;</div><div class="l">Quality (A)</div></button><button class="objtile" style="--acc:#8a9099" data-v="Z"><div class="ic">&#8853;</div><div class="n" data-cnt="Z">&#183;</div><div class="l">Position (Z)</div></button><button class="objtile" style="--acc:#E0651A" data-v="G"><div class="ic">&#9650;</div><div class="n" data-cnt="G">&#183;</div><div class="l">F&amp;G (GD)</div></button><button class="objtile" style="--acc:#15171A" data-v="H"><div class="ic">&#9705;</div><div class="n" data-cnt="H">&#183;</div><div class="l">Hand &amp; status</div></button></div><div class="note"><b>On the HMI, in no register and on no P&amp;ID:</b> <b>SV-341-1</b> (the slide valve, with the <b>Y1 Y4 Y2 Y3</b> solenoid indicators), <b>II-3411.1</b> (compressor motor current, A), <b>SD-341-1</b>, <b>341-ST-101.1</b> (suction strainer) and <b>PK-341-V1</b>, the vendor tag for V-341-1. <b>PDI-3414.x is a PLC calculation</b>, not a device &#8212; drawn as a square. Ranges printed from the register: <b>PT-3411.x 0..13.7895 bar abs</b> &#183; <b>PT-3412.x / PT-3413.x 0..34.47 barg</b> &#183; <b>PT-3401 0..28 barg</b>. <b>Manual Table 7.7 is misaligned in the source PDF</b> and is not quoted. Train 1 temperature transmitters read <b>&#8722;50/200 &#176;C</b> where trains 2&#8211;4 read <b>&#8722;50/120 &#176;C</b>, and the <b>operating-value column is empty for every Unit-340 instrument</b>.</div><div class="objmodal" id="imodal340"><div class="objbox"><div class="objhd"><span id="ititle340">&#8212;</span><button id="iclose340" aria-label="close">&#10005;</button></div><div class="objtblwrap" id="iwrap340"></div><div class="objfoot">Source: plant_instruments (local copy) &#183; Unit 340 &#183; range = calibrated span</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="6" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Start with what the register holds, then say honestly what it misses, because the two are not the same thing.", "The register holds exactly ONE control loop inside Unit 340: PIC-3401, receiver pressure acting on the E-342 fan motors, range 0 to 28 barg, on/off with hysteresis. Its set point is null in the database; Manual Table 7.9 gives 25 barg. Three more loops belong to the R1 cycle but sit in other areas &#8212; LIC-3111 in Unit 310, LIC-3540 and PIC-3552 in Unit 350.", "Now the correction. PIC-3411.x is REAL. There is a live PIC-3411.1 faceplate on the compressor HMI next to PIC-3552, so manual section 7.7.1 is right when it describes per-compressor suction pressure control, standalone or cascaded to the common master. What is wrong is the DATABASE, which has no record of these loops.", "The same is true of TIC-3417.1, the oil temperature controller: set points 50 and 70 degrees, mode ENABLED, visible on the HMI, and in no database table. Both loops are real and both are unregistered.", "PDI-3414.x is a different case and must not be lumped in with them: it is a PLC calculation from PT-3413.x and PT-3412.x, so there is nothing to register. PIC-3433.x, on the other hand, looks like a copy-paste from the Unit 350 chapter and is in no source but the manual text.", "Finally, PCV-3401 and PCV-3402 are self-acting regulators, not PCS loops: there is no faceplate to tune, only a set pressure on the valve."]'><div class="kicker">Operating interface &#183; loops <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual Table 7.9 &#183; plant_control_loops &#183; HMI Fig. 7.3 &#183; <b>1 registered loop, more on the screen</b></span></div><h2>Control loops and set points</h2><div class="mapwrap" style="padding:6px 10px 3px;"><div class="lbl"><span>Unit 340 &#8212; sensor to controller to final element</span><span class="you">&#8212; process tap &#183; &#8212;o&#8212; software link &#183; &#8212;&#8226;&#8594; electric 4-20 mA</span></div><div class="loc" data-circuit="loops340"></div></div><table class="data" style="font-size:10.4px;line-height:1.2;"><tr><th>Loop</th><th>Area</th><th>Duty</th><th>Final element</th><th>SP</th><th>Source</th></tr><tr><td><span class="k" style="color:var(--crimson);">PIC-3401</span></td><td><b>340</b></td><td>V-343 receiver pressure &#8212; on/off with hysteresis</td><td>E-342 fan motors</td><td><b>25 barg</b> (Manual 7.9)</td><td>register + manual</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">PIC-3411.x</span></td><td>340</td><td>per-compressor suction pressure &#8212; standalone or cascaded</td><td>slide valve</td><td>&#8212;</td><td><b>HMI + manual &#167;7.7.1 &#183; not in the DB</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">TIC-3417.1</span></td><td>340</td><td>oil temperature in V-341-x</td><td>X-341-x heater / cooler fans</td><td><b>50 / 70 &#176;C &#183; ENABLED</b></td><td><b>HMI only &#183; not in the DB</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">LIC-3111</span></td><td>310</td><td>E-311 shell ammonia level</td><td>LV-3111</td><td>35 %</td><td>register</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">LIC-3540</span></td><td>350</td><td>E-354 shell ammonia level</td><td>LV-3540</td><td>90 %</td><td>register</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">PIC-3552</span></td><td>350</td><td>R1 suction pressure &#8594; compressor capacity</td><td>slide valves (no element recorded)</td><td>&#8212;</td><td>register</td></tr></table><div class="cr340"><b>resolved</b><b>A-5 &#8212; RESOLVED.</b> <b>PIC-3411.x and TIC-3417.1 are real loops</b> with live faceplates on the compressor HMI; it is the <b>registry</b> that has no record of them, not the control system. <b>PDI-3414.x is a PLC calculation</b>, so no loop record is expected. <b>PIC-3433.x</b> remains unsupported by any source but the manual text. <b>PCV-3401 / PCV-3402</b> are self-acting regulators &#8212; no faceplate, only a set pressure.</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="4" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Five operating screens for this unit, Manual Figures 7.2 to 7.6, rotating &#8212; click any of them to enlarge.", "Screen 1, the overview, is the one to learn: the six condensers each with a fans-running counter, the PIC-3401 faceplate, PT-3401 and LT-3401 on the receiver, the two PCV set pressures, the four packages drawn as motor, compressor, separator, oil cooler and oil pump with a slide-valve percentage on each.", "Teach the reading order: bottom bar first &#8212; ALARMS, RESET, AUTO ALL &#8212; then the faceplate you care about, top down. The field to look at before you touch anything is NO PERM, which NAMES the permissive that is blocking a start. It is the fastest diagnosis on the unit.", "The compressor package screen, Figure 7.3, is where the lube-oil story is visible end to end: PT-3413.1 and PT-3412.1 with dashed lines into PDI-3414.1 in BarD, the PIC-3411.1 and TIC-3417.1 faceplates, the SV-341-1 slide valve with Y1 to Y4, and II-3411.1 motor current.", "Note the modes visible on the captures: PIC-3401 shows DISABLED, LIC-3111 shows OVERRIDE and LIC-3540 shows MANUAL. Those are as-captured states of a unit that is not running, not a recommended configuration.", "There is no HMI or screen table anywhere in the plant database &#8212; no faceplate definitions, no graphic pages. Everything on this slide comes from the manual captures, and that is the only source."]'><div class="kicker">Operating interface <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual &#167;7.6.1 &#183; Fig. 7.2&#8211;7.6 &#183; HMI philosophy 21044-AU-HMI-00002</span></div><h2>PCS operating screens &#8212; Unit 340</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1.6fr;"><ul class="points"><li><b>Fig. 7.2 &#8212; overview:</b> six condensers with <b>n FANS RUNNING</b>, <b>PIC-3401</b> faceplate, PT-3401 / LT-3401 on V-343, <b>PCV-3401 Set @ 25.45 Barg</b> and <b>PCV-3402 Set @ 24.7 Barg</b>, the two user loops.</li><li><b>Fig. 7.3 compressor package</b> &#8212; <b>PDI-3414.1 in BarD</b> fed by dashed lines from <b>PT-3413.1</b> and <b>PT-3412.1</b>, plus <b>PIC-3411.1</b>, <b>TIC-3417.1</b>, <b>SV-341-1</b> (Y1 Y4 Y2 Y3) and <b>II-3411.1</b> motor current.</li><li><b>7.4 oil system</b> &#183; <b>7.5 condensers</b> &#183; <b>7.6 auxiliary systems</b>.</li><li><b>Read the bottom bar first:</b> ALARMS &#183; RESET &#183; AUTO ALL &#183; and <b>NO PERM:</b>, which <b>names the permissive that is blocking the start</b>.</li><li>Modes on the capture: PIC-3401 <b>DISABLED</b>, LIC-3111 <b>OVERRIDE</b>, LIC-3540 <b>MANUAL</b> &#8212; as captured on a stopped unit.</li></ul><div class="carousel" id="hmi340" data-interval="5000"><a class="cimg active" href="/images/07-HMI-340-overview.png" target="_blank"><img src="/images/07-HMI-340-overview.png" alt="Unit 340 HMI overview (Manual Fig. 7.2)"></a><a class="cimg" href="/images/08-HMI-340-compressor.png" target="_blank"><img src="/images/08-HMI-340-compressor.png" alt="Unit 340 HMI compressor package (Manual Fig. 7.3)"></a><a class="cimg" href="/images/09-HMI-340-oilsystem.png" target="_blank"><img src="/images/09-HMI-340-oilsystem.png" alt="Unit 340 HMI oil system (Manual Fig. 7.4)"></a><a class="cimg" href="/images/10-HMI-340-condensers.png" target="_blank"><img src="/images/10-HMI-340-condensers.png" alt="Unit 340 HMI condensers (Manual Fig. 7.5)"></a><a class="cimg" href="/images/11-HMI-340-auxiliary.png" target="_blank"><img src="/images/11-HMI-340-auxiliary.png" alt="Unit 340 HMI auxiliary systems (Manual Fig. 7.6)"></a><div class="ccap">Auto-rotating &#183; click to enlarge</div><div class="cdots"><b class="active"></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b></div></div></div><div class="note">The plant database holds <b>no HMI or screen records at all</b> &#8212; no faceplate, no graphic page, no alarm-banner definition. Every screen shown here comes from the manual captures.</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="5" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Sixty alarm rows are registered for this unit: 39 alarms, 20 local shutdowns and one plant shutdown.", "Read the pattern rather than the list. Each package protects itself four ways &#8212; suction low-low, discharge high-high, discharge temperature high-high and oil differential low-low &#8212; and each of those trips ALL FOUR items of its own package: the compressor, its separator, its oil pump and its oil cooler. There is no cross-package effect anywhere in the matrix.", "That is the operating consequence worth saying: a trip here loses a third of the capacity, not the unit. The standby machine is what covers it, and starting it is a manual action.", "Hover the matrix to explore it. Be explicit about where it comes from: Unit 340 has NO digitised cause-and-effect in the database, so this matrix is built from Manual Table 7.11.", "And the gap that matters most: Table 7.10 lists PSD01, loss of ammonia loop, as the only plant shutdown of the unit &#8212; but Table 7.11 contains no effects for it. The unit has one PSD and no documented effect list for it.", "The vendor adds protections that this matrix does not carry at all &#8212; that is the next slide."]'><div class="kicker">Protection &#183; Alarms &amp; C&amp;E <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual Tables 7.10 / 7.11 &#183; 60 alarm rows &#183; interactive</span></div><h2>Critical alarms and the cause-and-effect matrix &#8212; hover to explore</h2><div id="ce340"></div><div class="note"><b>Unit 340 has no digitised cause-and-effect matrix</b> &#8212; plant_cause_effect returns no records for area 340, so the matrix above is built from <b>Manual Table 7.11</b> and is symmetric by package: each initiator trips <b>C-341-x, V-341-x, P-341-x and E-341-x</b> of its own package. <b>Table 7.11 contains no effects for PSD01</b> (loss of ammonia loop), the only PSD the unit has. TAHH-3419.x also carries a <b>null set point</b> in the register.</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="4" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["The alarm register tells you what trips. The vendor tells you what INHIBITS &#8212; and that is a different and equally important list, because most of these stop the machine before it ever runs.", "Low oil differential first: below 1.5 bar the automatic sequence halts and the compressor trips, to protect the bearings and the seals. It is both a start permissive and a running protection.", "Slide valve not unloaded: if the slide valve has not reached the fully unloaded position before the motor starts, the sequence is INHIBITED. A screw compressor started loaded is a repair job.", "Trip active: any existing trip &#8212; mechanical, electrical or process &#8212; prevents start-up or causes immediate shutdown. And oil temperature out of range delays the start until oil is back between 40 and 60 degrees.", "Then the one nobody has seen before: motor overcurrent. If motor current exceeds 535 amps, the PCS commands UNLOADING &#8212; it does not trip, it reduces the load. Flag it as an open point: this protection is in no manual chapter, no P&amp;ID and no alarm register, and 535 A is exactly the full-load current of the ABB M3GP 355LKB 2 in the motor specification table, so the number is credible.", "Last: if any permissive signal is lost DURING the start-up sequence, the system initiates an automatic safe shutdown. It does not continue and hope."]'><div class="kicker">Protection &#183; interlocks <span class="sub">&#8212; vendor manual slide 60 &#183; alarms and interlocks &#183; what INHIBITS a start</span></div><h2>Interlocks and protections</h2><table class="data" style="font-size:10.6px;line-height:1.24;"><tr><th>Interlock</th><th>Threshold</th><th>Action</th><th>Where it is documented</th></tr><tr><td><b>Low oil differential pressure</b></td><td><b>&lt; 1.5 bar</b> (PDI-3414.1)</td><td><b>sequence halts and the compressor trips</b></td><td>vendor + manual + register</td></tr><tr><td><b>Slide valve not unloaded</b></td><td>not at the fully unloaded position</td><td><b>start inhibited</b></td><td>vendor + Manual Table 7.13</td></tr><tr><td><b>Trip active</b></td><td>any mechanical, electrical or process trip</td><td><b>start prevented / immediate shutdown</b></td><td>vendor + manual</td></tr><tr><td><b>Motor overcurrent</b></td><td><b>&gt; 535 A</b></td><td><b>the PCS commands UNLOADING</b> &#8212; not a trip</td><td><b>vendor only</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Oil temperature out of range</b></td><td>outside <b>40&#8211;60 &#176;C</b></td><td><b>start delayed</b> until the band is regained</td><td>vendor</td></tr><tr><td><b>Permissive lost during start-up</b></td><td>any permissive signal lost mid-sequence</td><td><b>automatic safe shutdown</b></td><td>vendor</td></tr></table><div class="cr340"><b>open point</b><b>A-25 &#183; motor overcurrent &gt; 535 A &#8594; unload.</b> This protection appears in the vendor manual and <b>nowhere else</b> &#8212; not in a manual chapter, not on a P&amp;ID, not in the alarm register. <b>535 A is exactly the full-load current of the ABB M3GP 355LKB 2</b> compressor motor in plant_motor_model_specs, so the figure is credible. Reported, not adopted as a set point.</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="2" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["This is the safety layer that acts before anyone gets close enough to smell trouble.", "Five detectors: GD-05 on package 1, GD-06 on package 2, GD-07 on package 3, GD-08 on package 4 and GD-09 on the receiver. All of them carry the same note &#8212; under shelter above the compressor at roof elevation &#8212; because ammonia vapour rises and collects under a roof.", "The voting is 2ooN: two detectors in alarm are required before XAHH-3401 acts, and when it acts it takes a plant shutdown, not a local one. Two detectors, not one, so a single faulty head does not stop the plant &#8212; and a single detector in alarm is still a field response.", "Layout drawing is SAF11-ING-FR01. Know the field equipment too: emergency shower and spillage kit at E-342-4, extinguishers at E-342-1 and PK-341-1. The alarm pushbutton once shown at E-342-1 is marked REMOVED in the register.", "Response, in order and without improvisation: evacuate the area immediately, alert the control room, approach only upwind with the correct respiratory protection, and follow the emergency response procedure. Manual section 7 gives no isolation-through-a-cloud option, and neither do we."]'><div class="kicker">Protection &#183; gas detection <span class="sub">&#8212; XAHH-3401 &#183; SAF11-ING-FR01 &#183; plant_safety_devices</span></div><h2>Ammonia detection and leak response</h2><div class="grid2"><div><table class="data" style="font-size:10.8px;"><tr><th>Detector</th><th>Location</th><th>Note</th></tr><tr><td><span class="k">GD-05</span></td><td>PK-341-1</td><td>NOTE 2 &#183; under shelter above compressor, roof elevation</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">GD-06</span></td><td>PK-341-2</td><td>NOTE 2</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">GD-07</span></td><td>PK-341-3</td><td>NOTE 2</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">GD-08</span></td><td>PK-341-4</td><td>NOTE 2</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">GD-09</span></td><td>V-343 &#183; the receiver</td><td>NOTE 2</td></tr></table><div class="cr340"><b>as-read</b>The five detectors exist in the safety-device table only &#8212; they are <b>not registry assets</b>, so they do not appear in the discipline squares on slide 06.</div></div><div><div class="hcard eqc"><div class="hh"><span>&#9888; XAHH-3401</span><span>the only PSD of the unit</span></div><div class="hb"><div class="hval"><span class="k">Type</span><span class="v"><b>PSD</b> &#183; plant shutdown, not local</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Voting</span><span class="v"><b>2ooN</b> &#183; two detectors before it acts</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Initiators</span><span class="v">GD-05 &#183; GD-06 &#183; GD-07 &#183; GD-08 &#183; GD-09</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Drawing</span><span class="v">SAF11-ING-FR01</span></div><div class="hval"><span class="k">Effects</span><span class="v">not digitised &#8212; see slide 41</span></div></div></div><ul class="points"><li><b>On detection or on smell:</b> evacuate immediately, alert the control room, approach only upwind with the correct respiratory protection, then follow the emergency response procedure.</li><li><b>Field equipment:</b> emergency shower and spillage kit at <b>E-342-4</b>; extinguishers at <b>E-342-1</b> and <b>PK-341-1</b>; the E-342-1 alarm pushbutton is recorded <b>REMOVED</b>.</li></ul></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="5" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Start-up is a permissive list first and a sequence second. The vendor gives ten permissives, and if ANY of them is not satisfied the HMI shows the message Not ready to start and TERMINATES the start sequence. It does not wait, it does not retry.", "Read them as a picture of a ready machine: every compressor motor in AUTO, every oil pump in AUTO, every oil cooler fan in AUTO, the capacity slide valve in AUTO, suction and discharge valves open, all solenoid valves in AUTO, no active alarm or trip, the compressor unloaded below 5 per cent, the cool-down timers expired &#8212; and lube-oil temperature confirmed within 40 to 60 degrees.", "The vendor also gives the REASON for the waiting timer, which the manual does not: WARNING &#8212; starting the compressor filled with oil will damage the compressor, and the 20 minute time is to ensure the oil in the compressor is drained to the oil separator. The manual only says restart inhibit or cool-down timer expired, so the number and the reason are vendor-only.", "Then the sequence, and its order is the safety argument. Oil pump first to establish circulation and build differential. Prove oil differential at or above 1.5 bar. Drive the slide valve below 5 per cent and confirm the unloaded feedback. Only then start the main motor and let it reach nominal speed with no load.", "Then stabilise: slide valve automatically to about 15 per cent and hold there for 30 seconds. Oil cooler fans start and hold the oil between 40 and 60 degrees. The PCS enables the suction-pressure and capacity PIDs, and the machine reaches steady state under PIC-3552.", "One database note: plant_sequences holds SQ3401 to SQ3408 as titles only &#8212; no steps, no permissives, no timers. This slide is the manual and the vendor, not the register."]'><div class="kicker">Operation &#183; start-up <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual &#167;7.7.9 &#183; Table 7.13 &#183; vendor manual slides 55 / 56 / 57 &#183; Fig. 7.7 local panel</span></div><h2>Start-up permissives and sequence</h2><div class="grid2"><div><div class="collbl">The ten permissives &#8212; all must be confirmed</div><ul class="points"><li><b>a&#8211;d &#183; everything in AUTO:</b> all compressor motors (C-341-1/2/3 and the C-341-4 standby), all oil pumps, all oil cooler fans, and the capacity slide valve.</li><li><b>e &#183;</b> suction and discharge valves confirmed <b>OPEN</b>.</li><li><b>f &#183;</b> lube-oil temperature within the normal range, <b>40&#8211;60 &#176;C</b>.</li><li><b>g &#183;</b> the compressor is <b>unloaded &#8212; slide valve &lt; 5 %</b>.</li><li><b>h&#8211;j &#183;</b> all solenoid valves in AUTO, <b>no active alarm or trip</b>, and the <b>motor cool-down timers expired</b>.</li><li><b>If any one fails</b> the HMI shows <b>&#8220;Not ready to start&#8221;</b> and the sequence is <b>terminated</b> &#8212; the <b>NO PERM:</b> field names which one.</li></ul><div class="cr340 dgr"><b>warning</b><b>Starting a compressor filled with oil will damage the compressor.</b> The <b>20-minute</b> wait exists so the oil in the compressor drains back to the oil separator. The manual mentions only a restart-inhibit / cool-down timer &#8212; the number and the reason are <b>vendor-only (A-26)</b>.</div></div><div><div class="collbl">Sequence &#8212; Manual Table 7.13 + vendor slides 56 / 57</div><div class="flow"><div class="step"><div class="u">1 &#183; OIL PUMP</div><div class="t">Start <b>P-341-x</b> &#8212; establish circulation and build differential pressure.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">2 &#183; PROVE &#916;P</div><div class="t">PCS checks oil differential <b>&#8805; 1.5 bar</b> before proceeding.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">3 &#183; UNLOAD</div><div class="t">Slide valve driven <b>&lt; 5 %</b>; unloaded feedback confirmed on <b>ZT-3411.x</b>.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">4 &#183; MOTOR</div><div class="t">Start <b>MC-341-x</b> and accelerate to <b>nominal speed under no load</b>.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">5 &#183; STABILISE</div><div class="t">Slide valve automatically to <b>&#8776; 15 %</b> and hold <b>30 s</b>.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">6 &#183; OIL COOLING</div><div class="t">Oil-cooler fans start and hold the oil at <b>40&#8211;60 &#176;C</b>.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">7 &#183; CONTROL</div><div class="t">Enable the suction-pressure and capacity PIDs; steady state under <b>PIC-3552</b>.</div></div></div></div></div><div class="cr340"><b>gap</b><b>plant_sequences holds SQ3401&#8230;SQ3408 as titles only</b> &#8212; no steps, permissives or timers. <b>A-26:</b> the <b>20-minute oil-drain wait</b> and its reason are stated by the vendor only; the manual gives no number.</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="3" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Shutdown is the start-up run backwards, with one addition that people forget.", "Unload first, and the vendor names the solenoid: ZEV-3414 drives the slide valve to the fully unloaded position within the defined time frame. That reduces torque and mechanical load on the motor before it is stopped.", "Then stop the main motor &#8212; once the slide valve is fully unloaded and with suction and discharge pressures verified stable before the stop is confirmed.", "Then the addition: the oil pump KEEPS RUNNING for about 30 seconds of post-lubrication while the rotors coast down, lubricating the bearings and the seals, with oil differential pressure monitored to confirm circulation is still there.", "The oil pump stops once the post-lubrication timer expires and the differential has decayed safely. The oil check valves retain system priming and prevent backflow, which is why the next start does not begin dry. Finally the PCS verifies that the main motor contactor is de-energised and that no start command is active.", "And separately from all of this: there is an emergency stop push button on the package for emergency situations. It is not the sequence and it is not gentle &#8212; use it when the situation warrants it."]'><div class="kicker">Operation &#183; shutdown <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual Table 7.14 &#183; vendor manual slides 58 / 59 &#183; per package</span></div><h2>Shutdown sequence</h2><div class="flow"><div class="step"><div class="u">1 &#183; UNLOAD &#183; ZEV-3414</div><div class="t">The slide valve is commanded to the <b>fully unloaded</b> position within the defined time frame &#8212; this reduces torque and mechanical load on the motor before stopping.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">2 &#183; STOP MOTOR</div><div class="t">Once unloaded, the PCS stops the main motor. <b>Suction and discharge pressures are verified stable</b> before motor stop is confirmed.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">3 &#183; POST-LUBE &#8776; 30 s</div><div class="t"><b>The oil pump keeps running</b> to lubricate bearings and seals during coast-down; <b>oil &#916;P is monitored</b> to confirm continued circulation.</div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">4 &#183; STOP OIL PUMP</div><div class="t">Once the timer expires and the differential has <b>decayed safely</b>, the oil pump stops. <b>The oil check valves retain priming and prevent backflow.</b></div></div><div class="step"><div class="u">5 &#183; CONFIRM</div><div class="t">The PCS verifies the <b>main motor contactor is de-energised</b> and that <b>no start command is active</b>.</div></div></div><div class="grid2"><div><div class="note">A <b>trip</b> is not this sequence: an LSD stops the machine immediately and takes <b>C-341-x, V-341-x, P-341-x and E-341-x</b> of that package with it. The oil heater <b>X-341-x</b> is energised only when the compressor is shut down, which is what keeps a standby machine ready to start.</div></div><div><div class="cr340 dgr"><b>emergency</b>An <b>emergency stop push button</b> is provided on the package to stop the compressor in an emergency situation. It bypasses the ordered sequence above &#8212; expect no post-lubrication run and treat the machine as needing checks before restart.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="5" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["This slide used to say assembled, not quoted. It no longer does: the vendor publishes a Recommended Monitoring Schedule of 23 rows, and this is it.", "Structure it by interval and read the daily block as the round: check the oil level and LOG it, look for oil leaks, log oil pressure and oil temperature, check vibration, check ALL pressures and temperatures against the limitations and log them, check for leaks generally, and check the shaft seals for leakage. Seven of the eight daily items are on the oil system, which tells you what the vendor thinks breaks.", "Monthly is the same instruments looked at more carefully plus two new ones: an oil sample for quality, and motor lubrication. Bi-monthly, at 1 460 hours, is where you clean the strainer, grease the oil pump, check the safety controls and check the setting of the capacity control valve.", "Annually, at 8 760 hours: oil pump packing, examine the internal oil pump parts, coupling alignment, clean the suction strainer, tighten all bolts, and leak-test the whole compressor unit system.", "Three of the rows are condition-based rather than calendar-based, and they are the ones an operator triggers: change the filter at 10 psid, change the oil when the quality deteriorates, change the coalescers at 3 psid &#8212; and remember conflict A-23 on that last number.", "The second block is what the vendor table does NOT cover, because the vendor only knows its own skid: the receiver level and pressure, the condenser fan count, and the smell and ice checks on the process side of the loop."]'><div class="kicker">Maintenance &#183; the round <span class="sub">&#8212; vendor manual slide 62 &#8212; <b>Recommended Monitoring Schedule, 23 rows &#183; quoted, not assembled</b></span></div><h2>The monitoring schedule &#8212; daily to annual</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1.5fr 1fr;"><table class="data" style="font-size:10.2px;line-height:1.22;"><tr><th>Interval</th><th>What is checked</th></tr><tr><td><span class="k" style="color:var(--crimson);">DAILY &#183; 24 h</span></td><td>Check oil level <b>and log</b> &#183; check for oil leaks &#183; oil pressure <b>and log</b> &#183; oil temperature <b>and log</b> &#183; check vibration &#183; check <b>all pressures and temperatures against the limitations and log</b> &#183; check for leaks &#183; check the <b>shaft seals</b> for leakage</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">MONTHLY &#183; 720 h</span></td><td>Oil pressure &#183; oil temperature &#183; <b>oil sample quality</b> &#183; check motor lubrication</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">BI-MONTHLY &#183; 1 460 h</span></td><td><b>Clean strainer</b> &#183; grease the oil pump &#183; <b>check safety controls</b> &#183; check the setting of the <b>capacity control valve</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">ANNUALLY &#183; 8 760 h</span></td><td>Check oil pump packing &#183; examine internal oil pump parts &#183; <b>check coupling alignment</b> &#183; clean the suction strainer &#183; <b>tighten all bolts</b> &#183; <b>leak-test the compressor unit system</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k" style="color:var(--crimson);">CONDITION-BASED</span></td><td>Change the <b>filter</b> when the pressure drop reaches <b>10 psid</b> &#183; change the <b>oil</b> when its quality deteriorates &#183; change the <b>coalescers</b> when the pressure drop reaches <b>3 psid</b> (see A-23)</td></tr></table><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-monitoring-schedule.png" alt="Recommended monitoring schedule, vendor manual slide 62" style="max-height:186px;"><div class="cap">Vendor manual &#8212; the 23 rows</div></div></div><div class="note"><b>What the vendor table does not cover &#8212; the process side of the loop, and it is still your round:</b> <b>receiver level LT-3401</b>, logged every round, low alarm at <b>25 %</b>, a slow unexplained fall is a leak &#183; <b>receiver pressure PT-3401</b> between the <b>20</b> and <b>27 barg</b> alarms &#183; <b>condenser fans running per bank</b> on the HMI against the ambient conditions &#183; <b>ammonia smell, ice or oil traces</b> anywhere on the loop &#8212; report, do not investigate inside a cloud.</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="3" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Beyond the round there is a life plan for the machine, and the vendor publishes it as Table 5.9 against running hours: 300, 4 000, 8 000, 16 000, 24 000, 32 000 and 40 000.", "The screw compressor items are the ones with the longest reach. The shaft seal comes due at 16 000 hours. The thrust bearing and the main and side bearings come due at 32 000 hours. Alongside those there are unloader checks and noise and vibration checks at the shorter intervals.", "The oil pump has its own two: shaft seal at 16 000 hours and bearing metal at 24 000 hours. Read that next to the monitoring schedule, which greases the pump every two months and examines its internal parts annually &#8212; the two documents fit together.", "The rest of the table covers the coupling, the suction and discharge check valves, the lubrication-oil properties, the compressor motor bearings and an electrical check.", "One thing to read carefully is the legend: most entries are a CHECK made by a trained technician, with replacement only if required. It is not a replace-on-schedule list, and treating it as one wastes parts."]'><div class="kicker">Maintenance &#183; the life plan <span class="sub">&#8212; vendor manual slide 63 &#183; <b>Table 5.9</b> &#183; 300 / 4 000 / 8 000 / 16 000 / 24 000 / 32 000 / 40 000 h</span></div><h2>Preventive maintenance and overhaul</h2><div class="figrow" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1.15fr;"><table class="data" style="font-size:10.2px;line-height:1.22;"><tr><th>Component</th><th>Item</th><th>Hours</th></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Screw compressor</span></td><td><b>Shaft seal</b></td><td><b>16 000</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Screw compressor</span></td><td><b>Thrust bearing</b></td><td><b>32 000</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Screw compressor</span></td><td><b>Main bearing / side bearing</b></td><td><b>32 000</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Screw compressor</span></td><td>Unloader &#183; noise and vibration checks</td><td>shorter intervals</td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Oil pump</span></td><td><b>Shaft seal</b></td><td><b>16 000</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Oil pump</span></td><td><b>Bearing metal</b></td><td><b>24 000</b></td></tr><tr><td><span class="k">Also in the table</span></td><td>Coupling &#183; suction / discharge check valves &#183; lubrication-oil properties &#183; compressor motor bearings &#183; electrical check</td><td>&#8212;</td></tr></table><div class="note"><b>Read the legend before ordering parts:</b> most entries are <b>a check made by a trained technician, replace if required</b> &#8212; not a scheduled replacement.</div><div class="fig" style="margin-top:0;"><img src="/images/340-pm-overhaul.png" alt="Preventive maintenance and overhaul, vendor manual Table 5.9" style="max-height:212px;"><div class="cap">Vendor manual &#8212; Table 5.9</div></div></div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="4" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["The vendor troubleshooting table is more complete than manual section 7.8, so this is the vendor list, grouped so it can be used rather than read.", "Start with power, because it is the fastest thing to eliminate: complete loss of power means the main disconnect or the main fuse; partial loss means another fuse or the auxiliary supply. Then the oil pump: it will not run in HAND if the overloads are open, the panel control power is off, the motor is burnt out or the rotation is backwards &#8212; and remember the pumps must be in AUTO while the compressor is running.", "The pump runs in AUTO but the compressor will not start: lube-oil supply pressure low, so adjust the differential control valve to 30 to 60 psid; the compressor motor wired incorrectly; or the slide valve not below 5 per cent, which means checking the flow control valves and opening the hydraulic oil block valves.", "It starts and stops immediately: the motor starter auxiliary contact must reach the control panel within 3 SECONDS, so a wiring error there looks exactly like a start-logic error. Check both.", "Then the oil block, which is most of the table. Low lube-oil pressure: PDCV to 50 to 60 psid at the regulator inlet; oil too cold, so run the pump in hand mode with the compressor suction valve closed to warm it to at least 90 degrees Fahrenheit; a defective or wrongly set pressure switch; a clogged filter; or simply a low level in the reservoir.", "And the four that tell you something is loose: high oil carryover means a loose coalescer; high filter differential means a choked coalescer drain line or a plugged filter; a falling reservoir level means leaks, loose coalescers, a blocked oil return line, compressor seal leakage or oil leaving through a relief valve; and abnormal noise means bearings, loose connections, foreign material, misalignment or rotors rubbing the casing."]'><div class="kicker">Operation &#183; troubleshooting <span class="sub">&#8212; vendor manual slides 64&#8211;66 &#8212; <b>22 symptoms</b>, more complete than Manual &#167;7.8</span></div><h2>Troubleshooting</h2><table class="data" style="font-size:9.4px;line-height:1.16;"><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Possible cause</th><th>Remedy</th></tr><tr><td><b>Complete / partial loss of power</b></td><td>Main disconnect open or main fuse blown &#183; another fuse &#183; auxiliary supply disconnected</td><td>Check all power wiring &#183; replace the burned-out fuse &#183; check wiring to the auxiliary equipment</td></tr><tr><td><b>Oil pump will not run in HAND</b></td><td>Overloads open &#183; the compressor is running &#183; main power off &#183; panel control power off &#183; motor burned out &#183; rotation backwards</td><td>Reset &#183; <b>the pumps must be in AUTO while the compressor runs</b> &#183; check the starter disconnect &#183; turn panel power on &#183; check and correct rotation</td></tr><tr><td><b>Oil pump will not run in AUTO</b></td><td>Compressor start not initiated &#183; pump not putting out enough pressure &#183; oil cold &#183; rotation backwards</td><td>The pumps do not run until start is initiated &#183; <b>adjust the PDCV to 30&#8211;60 psid</b> &#183; warm the oil &#183; correct the rotation</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pump runs in AUTO but the compressor will not start</b></td><td>Lube-oil supply pressure low &#183; compressor motor wired incorrectly &#183; <b>slide valve not &lt; 5 %</b></td><td><b>Adjust the PDCV to 30&#8211;60 psid</b> &#183; check and correct the motor wiring &#183; check the flow control valves and open the hydraulic oil block valves</td></tr><tr><td><b>Starts then stops immediately</b></td><td>Starter or auxiliary contact wired incorrectly &#183; error in the compressor start logic</td><td><b>The auxiliary contact must reach the control panel within 3 seconds</b> &#183; check and correct the start logic</td></tr><tr><td><b>High / low lube-oil temperature</b></td><td>High suction temperature &#183; thermal expansion valve not functioning properly</td><td>Check and correct</td></tr><tr><td><b>Low lube-oil pressure</b></td><td>Pump not putting out enough pressure &#183; oil too cold &#183; defective or wrongly set pressure switch &#183; loose contact wiring &#183; clogged lube-oil filter &#183; low level in the reservoir</td><td><b>Adjust the PDCV to 50&#8211;60 psid at the regulator inlet</b> &#183; <b>run the pump in hand mode to warm the oil to at least 90 &#176;F with the compressor suction valve closed</b> &#183; check the switch setting &#183; replace the filter &#183; add oil to the vessel</td></tr><tr><td><b>Bad oil quality</b></td><td>Solids in the oil</td><td>Drain and refill with clean oil, flush if necessary, <b>have the oil analysed</b> and find the source of the solids</td></tr><tr><td><b>High oil carryover</b></td><td><b>Coalescer element loose</b></td><td>The coalescer must be tight and correctly mounted &#183; check the differential across it (see conflict <b>A-23</b>)</td></tr><tr><td><b>High &#916;P across the oil filter</b></td><td>Coalescer drain line choked &#183; oil filter plugged</td><td>Look for oil accumulating in the sight glass and open the globe valve for proper oil flow &#183; replace the filter</td></tr><tr><td><b>Falling level in the oil reservoir</b></td><td>Oil leaks &#183; coalescers not tight enough to seal &#183; oil return line from the separator blocked &#183; excess leakage from the compressor seal &#183; <b>oil leaving through a relief valve</b></td><td>Check and correct &#183; tighten the coalescers &#183; adjust the block valve &#183; <b>check all relief valve discharge piping for oil</b></td></tr><tr><td><b>Abnormal noise</b></td><td>Damaged bearings &#183; loose connections &#183; foreign material in the compressor &#183; misalignment or damaged coupling &#183; <b>rotors rubbing the casing</b></td><td>Replace the bearings &#183; stop and tighten &#183; stop and check the suction strainer and internals &#183; check alignment and coupling &#183; stop and disassemble</td></tr></table></section>
<section class="slide m101docs" data-min="3" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["The document shelf of the unit &#8212; the same register the engineering viewer reads.", "Start with the vendor pre-read list, because it is a short and specific instruction: the operator shall be familiar with the P&amp;ID, the general arrangement drawing, the process flow diagram, the control philosophy, the alarm and set-point list and the operation and maintenance manual BEFORE the training session. Six documents, no more.", "Then the register itself. Five P&amp;IDs: PR34 to PR37 for the four packages and PR38 for the common system. Plus the utility flow diagram PRO10-ING-PR07, which is the drawing this whole module is built on, and now the vendor package manual TAM-158.", "Two revision facts worth knowing before you quote a drawing: PR34 and PR38 are at revision 7 in the register, while PR35, PR36 and PR37 are at revision 1, and the training brief cites PR38 rev 8 &#8212; the register is one revision behind the drawing.", "All five P&amp;IDs are marked not available with no published URL, so the buttons on this slide will grey themselves out if the file is not on the site. That is the register being honest, not a broken link."]'><div class="kicker">Reference &#183; Documents <span class="sub">&#8212; Manual Table 7.2 &#183; plant_documents &#183; vendor manual slide 5</span></div><h2>Unit 340 &#8212; relevant documents</h2><div class="note"><b>Read before the session</b> &#8212; the vendor&#8217;s own pre-read list: <b>1</b> piping &amp; instrument diagram (P&amp;ID) &#183; <b>2</b> general arrangement drawing &#183; <b>3</b> process flow diagram &#183; <b>4</b> control philosophy &#183; <b>5</b> alarm &amp; set-points list &#183; <b>6</b> operation &amp; maintenance manual.</div><div class="docbtns"><a class="docbtn" href="/eni/#doc/PRO13-ING-PR38" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>PR38 &#8212; Refrigeration Unit 340</b><span>21044-PR-PID-34005 &#183; common system &#183; rev 7 in the register</span></a><a class="docbtn" href="/eni/#doc/PRO13-ING-PR34" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>PR34 &#8212; Package PK-341-1</b><span>21044-PR-PID-34001 &#183; 2 sheets &#183; rev 7</span></a><a class="docbtn" href="/eni/#doc/PRO13-ING-PR35" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>PR35 &#8212; Package PK-341-2</b><span>21044-PR-PID-34002 &#183; rev 1</span></a><a class="docbtn" href="/eni/#doc/PRO13-ING-PR36" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>PR36 &#8212; Package PK-341-3</b><span>21044-PR-PID-34003 &#183; rev 1</span></a><a class="docbtn" href="/eni/#doc/PRO13-ING-PR37" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>PR37 &#8212; Package PK-341-4</b><span>21044-PR-PID-34004 &#183; rev 1</span></a></div><table class="data" id="docstbl102" style="font-size:12px;"></table><div class="note">Manual Table 7.2 also lists <b>PRO02-ING-FR02</b> process description, <b>PRO10-ING-PR07</b> utility flow diagram, <b>SAF01-ING-FR01</b> isolation philosophy, <b>SAF12-ING-FR01</b> cause and effect, <b>SAF05-ING-FR01</b> relief and blowdown and <b>21044-AU-HMI-00002</b> HMI philosophy. Held locally but not in Table 7.2: <b>TAM-158 Training Manual 340 rev 1</b>, the vendor package manual behind most of Block 2 and the maintenance slides, the user P&amp;IDs <b>PR08</b> and <b>PR22</b>, the <b>NH&#8323; MSDS</b>, the <b>TAM-158 NH&#8323; HSE</b> deck and the Unit-340 alarm list. All five Unit-340 P&amp;IDs are registered <b>not available</b>.</div></section>
<section class="slide" data-min="4" data-block="Block 4" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Key messages &#8212; the exam blueprint condensed. Read them slowly and let people write.", "If they remember one sentence, make it the flow path. If they remember one number set, make it the pressure ladder. If they remember one hazard, make it liquid carry-over to the compressors.", "The oil story is the new centre of gravity of this module, so give it three sentences: the differential that starts and trips the machine is CALCULATED from two transmitters and must always stay above 1.5 bar D; there are two oil circuits sharing one cooler, five fans each; and the magnetic coupling can decouple silently, so the differential is the only thing that will tell you.", "The maintenance intervals are new and they are examinable: daily oil log and vibration, monthly oil sample, bi-monthly strainer and capacity valve, annual bolts and leak test, filter at 10 psid, oil at 16 000 hours with the first change at 8 000, shaft seals at 16 000, bearings at 32 000.", "Close by pointing forward: Module 104 is Unit 350 liquefaction, and E-354 &#8212; the biggest consumer of everything we saw today &#8212; belongs to it."]'><div class="kicker">Take-home &#183; Recap</div><h2>Key messages &#8212; Module 103</h2><ul class="points"><li><b>1 &#183;</b> The whole flow path in one sentence: <b>suction header &#8594; PK-341-1&#8230;4 &#8594; PCV-3401 &#8594; E-342-1&#8230;6 &#8594; V-343 &#8594; LV-3111 / LV-3540 &#8594; E-311 / E-354 &#8594; back to the suction header</b>. One fluid, four states, never mixed with the gas.</li><li><b>2 &#183;</b> The machine: <b>Grasso VR-V30T-28</b> oil-flooded screw, 560 kW, 2 950 rpm, <b>positive displacement &#8212; it never stops pumping</b>, which is why PSV-3411.x exists. Capacity is the <b>slide valve</b>, driven hydraulically <b>by the compressor oil</b> from PT-3552.</li><li><b>3 &#183;</b> <b>Oil is the machine.</b> <b>PDI-3414.1 is CALCULATED</b> in the PLC as <b>PT-3413.1 &#8722; PT-3412.1</b> and must <b>ALWAYS stay above 1.5 bar D</b>; two circuits (injection and functional) share <b>one cooler, 5 fans each</b>; the <b>magnetic coupling can decouple silently</b>, and the differential is the only thing that will tell you.</li><li><b>4 &#183;</b> The oil numbers worth memorising: separator levels <b>350 / 220 / 110 mm</b> from the bottom, oil band <b>40&#8211;60 &#176;C</b>, filter change at <b>0.7&#8211;1 barg (10 psid)</b>, <b>4 coalescers</b> whose return line goes back to <b>suction</b> and whose needle valves are a <b>one-time commissioning setting &#8212; do not touch them</b>.</li><li><b>5 &#183;</b> Maintenance: <b>daily</b> oil level, pressure, temperature, vibration and all pressures logged &#183; <b>monthly</b> oil sample &#183; <b>bi-monthly</b> strainer, pump grease, safety controls, capacity valve &#183; <b>annual</b> bolts, alignment, leak test &#183; oil change at <b>16 000 h</b> (first at <b>8 000 h</b>) &#183; shaft seals <b>16 000 h</b>, bearings <b>32 000 h</b>.</li><li><b>6 &#183;</b> <b>Compressor selection is manual</b> &#8212; 3 working + 1 standby, started from the panel. Ten permissives, <b>&#8220;Not ready to start&#8221;</b> and the <b>NO PERM:</b> field name the blocker; <b>starting a compressor full of oil will damage it</b>, which is what the 20-minute wait is for. Ammonia is <b>toxic and corrosive</b>: <b>XAHH-3401, 2ooN, takes a plant shutdown</b>, and the unit has <b>no SDV and no BDV</b>.</li><li><b>7 &#183;</b> Open points now on the record: <b>A-22</b> slide-valve range 20&#8211;100 % vs 15&#8211;30 % vs &lt;5 %/15 % &#183; <b>A-23</b> coalescer &#916;P 0.8 barg vs 3 psid vs 0.25 psid &#183; <b>A-24</b> the 5+5 fan split &#183; <b>A-25</b> the <b>535 A unload</b> that is in no other source &#183; <b>A-26</b> the 20-minute oil-drain wait &#183; and the standing <b>E-342 fan count 12 vs 18</b> and <b>fan voltage 400 vs 690 V</b>. <b>Closed this revision: A-5 and A-6.</b></li></ul><div class="note">Next: <b>Module 104 &#8212; Unit 350 Liquefaction I</b>, which owns <b>E-354</b>, the evaporator that takes about ninety per cent of everything this unit produces.</div></section>
<section class="slide inter brk" data-min="15" data-block="Break" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Second 15-minute break before questions and answers. Give the exact return time.", "Ask the room to write down the questions they want answered against a drawing &#8212; PR38 and the two user P&amp;IDs are the ones to have open.", "Remind them the exam covers the objectives and key messages only."]'><div><div class="big">&#9749; 15:00</div><h2>Break</h2><p>15 minutes. Q&amp;A follows, then the module exam and evaluation.</p></div></section>
<section class="slide inter" data-min="15" data-block="Q&amp;A" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Open discussion &#8212; 15 minutes.", "Collect anything that needs a drawing on the screen and answer it against PR38, PR34 or the user P&amp;IDs rather than from memory.", "Log any question that exposes a documentation conflict &#8212; several are already open on this unit, and field feedback is what closes them."]'><div><div class="big">?</div><h2>Questions &amp; Answers</h2><p>15 minutes. Open discussion before the assessment.</p></div></section>
<section class="slide inter" data-min="30" data-block="Assessment" data-presenter="" data-notes='["Module 103 exam: it covers the objectives and the key messages only, nothing outside them.", "Remind the group that where two documents disagree, the exam asks what the deck printed and why it was flagged &#8212; not for a number nobody can prove.", "The module evaluation form follows the exam; both are needed to close the module record."]'><div><div class="big">30:00</div><h2>Exam + Module Evaluation</h2><p>Module 103 assessment covering the objectives and key messages, followed by the trainee evaluation form.</p></div></section>
  </div>


  <div class="navbar">
    <div class="counter"><span id="cur">01</span> / <span id="total">00</span></div>
    <div class="navmid">
      <button class="nav" id="present">⧉ Presenter view</button>
      <button class="nav" id="fs">⛶ Fullscreen</button>
      <button class="nav" id="printpdf">🖨 Print / Export PDF</button>
    </div>
    <div class="navbtns">
      <button class="nav" id="prev">‹ Prev</button>
      <button class="nav primary" id="next">Next ›</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="progress"><i id="bar"></i></div>

</div>

<script>
  const slides=[...document.querySelectorAll('.slide')];
  let i=0;
  const cur=document.getElementById('cur'), bar=document.getElementById('bar'),
        prev=document.getElementById('prev'), next=document.getElementById('next'),
        present=document.getElementById('present'), fs=document.getElementById('fs'),
        printpdf=document.getElementById('printpdf'),
        deckEl=document.querySelector('.deck');
  document.getElementById('total').textContent=String(slides.length).padStart(2,'0');

  const fmt=m=>{const h=Math.floor(m/60),mm=m%60;return h+':' + String(mm).padStart(2,'0');};
  let acc=0; const data=slides.map(s=>{
    const m=parseInt(s.dataset.min||'0',10); const start=acc; acc+=m;
    let notes=[]; try{notes=JSON.parse(s.dataset.notes||'[]');}catch(e){}
    // textContent pega los dos lados de un <br> sin espacio ("Unit 200 —LNG Plant
    // Inlet" en la portada). El titulo va a la vista de presentador, asi que el
    // salto de linea tiene que valer como espacio.
    const th=(s.querySelector('h1,h2')||{}).innerHTML||'';
    const t=th.replace(/<br\s*\/?>/gi,' ').replace(/<[^>]*>/g,'')
              .replace(/&amp;/g,'&').replace(/&nbsp;/g,' ');
    return {title:t.replace(/\s+/g,' ').trim(), block:s.dataset.block||'', presenter:s.dataset.presenter||'', min:m, start:start, end:acc, notes:notes};
  });
  const TOTAL=acc;

  let bc=null; try{ bc=('BroadcastChannel' in window)? new BroadcastChannel('tendrara-mod103'):null; }catch(e){ bc=null; }
  let presenterWin=null;
  function sendToPresenter(msg){
    if(bc){ try{ bc.postMessage(msg); }catch(e){} }
    if(presenterWin && !presenterWin.closed){ try{ presenterWin.postMessage(msg,'*'); }catch(e){} }
  }

  function render(){
    slides.forEach((s,k)=>s.classList.toggle('active',k===i));
    cur.textContent=String(i+1).padStart(2,'0');
    bar.style.width=((i+1)/slides.length*100)+'%';
    prev.disabled=(i===0); next.disabled=(i===slides.length-1);
    slides[i].scrollTop=0;
    sendToPresenter({type:'state', index:i});
  }
  function show(n){i=Math.max(0,Math.min(slides.length-1,n));render();}

  prev.onclick=()=>show(i-1); next.onclick=()=>show(i+1);
  document.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{
    if(e.target.tagName==='INPUT')return;
    if(e.key==='ArrowRight'||e.key==='PageDown'||e.key===' ')show(i+1);
    if(e.key==='ArrowLeft'||e.key==='PageUp')show(i-1);
    if(e.key==='Home')show(0); if(e.key==='End')show(slides.length-1);
    if(e.key.toLowerCase()==='f')toggleFs();
  });

  function toggleFs(){
    const root=document.documentElement;
    if(!document.fullscreenElement){ (root.requestFullscreen||root.webkitRequestFullscreen).call(root); }
    else { (document.exitFullscreen||document.webkitExitFullscreen).call(document); }
  }
  fs.onclick=toggleFs;
  printpdf.onclick=()=>window.print();

  // ---- Fullscreen auto-fit: the deck is a 1040x720 canvas scaled uniformly to the screen ----
  // (Fullscreen goes on <html>: the Fullscreen spec forces transform:none / width:100% !important
  //  on the fullscreened element itself, so the deck must be a child to be scalable.)
  const BASE_W=1040, BASE_H=720;
  function applyFit(){
    const on=!!document.fullscreenElement;
    document.body.classList.toggle('fsmode',on);
    if(on){
      const z=Math.min(innerWidth/BASE_W, innerHeight/BASE_H);
      deckEl.style.width=BASE_W+'px';
      deckEl.style.height=BASE_H+'px';
      deckEl.style.maxWidth='none';
      deckEl.style.transformOrigin='center center';
      deckEl.style.transform='scale('+z+')';
    }else{
      deckEl.style.width='';deckEl.style.height='';deckEl.style.maxWidth='';
      deckEl.style.transform='';deckEl.style.transformOrigin='';
    }
  }
  window.addEventListener('resize',applyFit);
  document.addEventListener('fullscreenchange',()=>{
    const on=!!document.fullscreenElement;
    fs.classList.toggle('on',on);
    fs.textContent= on ? '⛶ Exit full screen' : '⛶ Fullscreen';
    applyFit();
  });

  function onCmd(m){ m=m||{};
    if(m.type==='goto') show(m.index);
    else if(m.type==='nav') show(i + (m.dir||0));
    else if(m.type==='hello') render();
  }
  if(bc) bc.onmessage=(ev)=>onCmd(ev.data);
  window.addEventListener('message',(ev)=>onCmd(ev.data));

  present.onclick=openPresenter;
  function openPresenter(){
    presenterWin=window.open('', 'tendrara_presenter102', 'width=560,height=720');
    if(!presenterWin){ alert('Please allow pop-ups to open the presenter view.'); return; }
    presenterWin.document.open();
    presenterWin.document.write(presenterHTML());
    presenterWin.document.close();
    setTimeout(()=>{ sendToPresenter({type:'state', index:i}); }, 300);
    setTimeout(()=>{ sendToPresenter({type:'state', index:i}); }, 1000);
  }

  function presenterHTML(){
    const DATA=JSON.stringify(data), TOT=TOTAL;
    return `<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Module 103 — Presenter</title>
<style>
  :root{--crimson:#C8102E;--blue:#4d9fe8;--amber:#E0A106;--ink:#e9eaec;--mut:#9a9da3;--line:#2a2d33;}
  *{box-sizing:border-box;}
  body{margin:0;font-family:"Segoe UI",Arial,sans-serif;background:#161819;color:var(--ink);}
  .top{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;padding:12px 16px;
    border-bottom:2px solid var(--crimson);background:#1b1d21;position:sticky;top:0;}
  .top .ttl{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mut);font-family:"Consolas",monospace;}
  .clock{font-family:"Consolas",monospace;font-size:13px;color:var(--ink);}
  .clock b{color:#fff;} .clock .ahead{color:#39c46e;} .clock .behind{color:#ff5b5b;}
  .timerbtns button{font-size:11px;border:1px solid var(--line);background:#23262b;color:var(--ink);
    border-radius:4px;padding:4px 8px;cursor:pointer;margin-left:4px;}
  .body{padding:16px 18px 90px;}
  .meta{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap;font-family:"Consolas",monospace;font-size:12px;color:var(--mut);margin-bottom:8px;}
  .badge{background:var(--crimson);color:#fff;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:3px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.04em;}
  .badge.blk{background:#33363c;color:#cfd2d7;}
  .badge.pr{background:#3a1f25;color:#ff98a6;}          /* Ivan — crimson */
  .badge.pr.mm{background:#16293c;color:var(--blue);}    /* Mauro — blue */
  .badge.pr.az{background:#15301f;color:#4ec97e;}        /* Alessandro — green */
  .badge.pr.both{background:#33363c;color:#cfd2d7;}      /* Both / none */
  .cur-title{font-size:23px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.2;margin:2px 0 4px;color:#fff;}
  .times{font-family:"Consolas",monospace;font-size:12px;color:var(--mut);margin-bottom:14px;}
  .times b{color:#fff;}
  h4{margin:14px 0 6px;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--crimson);}
  ul{margin:0;padding-left:20px;} li{font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;margin:7px 0;color:#dfe1e5;}
  .next{margin-top:18px;padding:12px 14px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:6px;background:#1b1d21;}
  .next .lbl{font-family:"Consolas",monospace;font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mut);}
  .next .nt{font-size:15px;color:#cfd2d7;margin-top:3px;}
  .nav{position:fixed;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;display:flex;gap:10px;padding:12px 16px;background:#1b1d21;border-top:1px solid var(--line);}
  .nav button{flex:1;font-size:15px;font-weight:600;border:1px solid var(--line);background:#23262b;color:var(--ink);
    border-radius:5px;padding:11px;cursor:pointer;}
  .nav button.p{background:var(--crimson);border-color:var(--crimson);color:#fff;}
  .nav button:disabled{opacity:.4;cursor:not-allowed;}
  .pbar{height:3px;background:#23262b;} .pbar i{display:block;height:100%;background:var(--crimson);}
  .tl{margin:6px 0 16px;}
  .tllabel{font-family:"Consolas",monospace;font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mut);margin-bottom:7px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;}
  .tllabel .leg b{color:#fff;} .tllabel .leg .a{color:#39c46e;} .tllabel .leg .p{color:#fff;}
  .tlbar{position:relative;height:30px;border-radius:5px;overflow:visible;display:flex;background:#23262b;}
  .seg{height:30px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-family:"Consolas",monospace;font-size:9px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.65);border-right:1px solid #161819;overflow:hidden;white-space:nowrap;}
  .seg:last-child{border-right:none;}
  .seg.c{background:#3a1f25;} .seg.b{background:#4a3a16;} .seg.q{background:#22323a;} .seg.x{background:#2c2230;}
  .tlslide{position:absolute;top:0;height:30px;border:2px solid #fff;border-radius:3px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.12);pointer-events:none;transition:left .2s,width .2s;z-index:2;}
  .tlnow{position:absolute;top:-5px;height:40px;width:2px;background:#39c46e;pointer-events:none;display:none;z-index:3;}
  .tlnow:before{content:"▲";position:absolute;top:-13px;left:50%;transform:translateX(-50%);color:#39c46e;font-size:11px;}
  .tlticks{position:relative;height:13px;margin-top:3px;}
  .tlticks span{position:absolute;transform:translateX(-50%);font-family:"Consolas",monospace;font-size:9px;color:var(--mut);white-space:nowrap;}
</style></head><body>
  <div class="top">
    <span class="ttl">Module 103 · Presenter</span>
    <span class="clock" id="clock">⏱ <b id="elapsed">0:00</b> <span id="delta"></span></span>
    <span class="timerbtns"><button id="tStart">Start</button><button id="tReset">Reset</button></span>
  </div>
  <div class="pbar"><i id="pbar"></i></div>
  <div class="body">
    <div class="meta"><span class="badge" id="num">--</span><span class="badge blk" id="blk">--</span><span class="badge pr" id="pres">--</span></div>
    <div class="cur-title" id="title">—</div>
    <div class="times" id="times"></div>
    <div class="tl">
      <div class="tllabel"><span id="tlrange">Schedule</span>
        <span class="leg"><span class="a">▲ where you are</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <span class="p">▮ where you should be</span></span></div>
      <div class="tlbar" id="tlbar"><div class="tlslide" id="tlslide"></div><div class="tlnow" id="tlnow"></div></div>
      <div class="tlticks" id="tlticks"></div>
    </div>
    <h4>Talking points</h4>
    <ul id="notes"></ul>
    <div class="next"><div class="lbl">Next</div><div class="nt" id="nextt">—</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="nav">
    <button id="prev">‹ Prev</button>
    <button class="p" id="next">Next ›</button>
  </div>
<script>
  const DATA=${DATA}, TOTAL=${TOT};
  const fmt=m=>{const h=Math.floor(m/60),mm=m%60;return h+':'+String(mm).padStart(2,'0');};
  let bc=null; try{ bc=new BroadcastChannel('tendrara-mod103'); }catch(e){ bc=null; }
  function send(m){ if(bc){ try{ bc.postMessage(m); }catch(e){} } try{ if(window.opener && !window.opener.closed) window.opener.postMessage(m,'*'); }catch(e){} }
  let idx=0;
  document.getElementById('tlrange').textContent='Schedule · 0:00 → '+fmt(TOTAL);
  (function buildTimeline(){
    const norm=b=> b==='Module open'?'Block 1':b;
    const clsOf=b=>({'Block 1':'c','Block 2':'c','Block 3':'c','Break':'b','Q&A':'q','Assessment':'x'}[b]||'c');
    const segs=[];
    DATA.forEach(d=>{const nb=norm(d.block);const last=segs[segs.length-1];
      if(last&&last.block===nb){last.min+=d.min;last.end+=d.min;}
      else segs.push({block:nb,min:d.min,start:(last?last.end:0),end:(last?last.end:0)+d.min});});
    let html='';segs.forEach(s=>{const w=(s.min/TOTAL*100).toFixed(3);
      html+='<div class="seg '+clsOf(s.block)+'" style="width:'+w+'%">'+(s.min>=20?s.block:'')+'</div>';});
    document.getElementById('tlbar').insertAdjacentHTML('afterbegin',html);
    const bounds=[0];segs.forEach(s=>bounds.push(s.end));
    document.getElementById('tlticks').innerHTML=bounds.map(b=>'<span style="left:'+(b/TOTAL*100)+'%">'+fmt(b)+'</span>').join('');
  })();
  function paint(){
    const d=DATA[idx];
    document.getElementById('num').textContent=String(idx+1).padStart(2,'0')+'/'+String(DATA.length).padStart(2,'0');
    document.getElementById('blk').textContent=d.block;
    const pe=document.getElementById('pres');
    pe.textContent='▸ '+(d.presenter||'—');
    // Los tres instructores de ESTE modulo, con el mismo color que la slide 03.
    // Antes esto miraba un nombre — «Zibac» — que es del modulo 002 y no aparece
    // en ninguna slide de aqui, asi que el color de segundo ponente no se
    // encendia nunca.
    var PR={'':' both','Mario':'','Mauro':' mm','Lorenzo':' az','Both':' both','—':' both'};
    pe.className='badge pr'+(PR[d.presenter]!==undefined?PR[d.presenter]:'');
    document.getElementById('title').textContent=d.title;
    document.getElementById('times').innerHTML='This slide <b>'+d.min+' min</b> · runs <b>'+fmt(d.start)+'</b> → <b>'+fmt(d.end)+'</b> · of '+fmt(TOTAL);
    document.getElementById('notes').innerHTML=d.notes.length? d.notes.map(n=>'<li>'+n+'</li>').join('') : '<li style="color:#777">No notes.</li>';
    document.getElementById('nextt').textContent= DATA[idx+1]? (DATA[idx+1].presenter?'['+DATA[idx+1].presenter+'] ':'')+DATA[idx+1].title : '— end of module —';
    document.getElementById('pbar').style.width=((idx+1)/DATA.length*100)+'%';
    document.getElementById('prev').disabled=(idx===0);
    document.getElementById('next').disabled=(idx===DATA.length-1);
    document.getElementById('tlslide').style.left=(DATA[idx].start/TOTAL*100)+'%';
    document.getElementById('tlslide').style.width=(DATA[idx].min/TOTAL*100)+'%';
    updateDelta();
  }
  function onState(m){ if(m&&m.type==='state'){ idx=m.index; paint(); } }
  if(bc) bc.onmessage=(ev)=>onState(ev.data);
  window.addEventListener('message',(ev)=>onState(ev.data));
  document.getElementById('prev').onclick=()=>send({type:'goto',index:Math.max(0,idx-1)});
  document.getElementById('next').onclick=()=>send({type:'goto',index:Math.min(DATA.length-1,idx+1)});
  document.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{
    if(e.key==='ArrowRight'||e.key===' ')send({type:'goto',index:Math.min(DATA.length-1,idx+1)});
    if(e.key==='ArrowLeft')send({type:'goto',index:Math.max(0,idx-1)});
  });
  let running=false, startedAt=0, accumSec=0, raf=null;
  function curSec(){ return Math.floor(accumSec + (running ? (Date.now()-startedAt)/1000 : 0)); }
  function tick(){
    const sec=curSec();
    document.getElementById('elapsed').textContent=Math.floor(sec/60)+':'+String(sec%60).padStart(2,'0');
    const now=document.getElementById('tlnow'); now.style.display='block';
    now.style.left=Math.min(100,(sec/60)/TOTAL*100)+'%';
    updateDelta(sec);
  }
  function updateDelta(sec){
    const el=document.getElementById('delta');
    if(!running && accumSec===0){el.textContent='';return;}
    if(sec===undefined) sec=curSec();
    const m=sec/60, d=DATA[idx];
    if(m > d.end) el.innerHTML='<span class="behind">+'+Math.round(m-d.end)+'m behind</span>';
    else if(m < d.start) el.innerHTML='<span class="ahead">'+Math.round(d.start-m)+'m ahead</span>';
    else el.innerHTML='<span style="color:#39c46e">on pace</span>';
  }
  document.getElementById('tStart').onclick=function(){
    if(!running){ running=true; startedAt=Date.now(); this.textContent='Pause'; raf=setInterval(tick,250); tick(); }
    else { running=false; accumSec=curSec(); clearInterval(raf); this.textContent='Resume'; updateDelta(accumSec); }
  };
  document.getElementById('tReset').onclick=function(){
    running=false; accumSec=0; clearInterval(raf);
    document.getElementById('elapsed').textContent='0:00';
    document.getElementById('delta').textContent='';
    document.getElementById('tStart').textContent='Start';
    document.getElementById('tlnow').style.display='none';
  };
  send({type:'hello'});
  paint();
<\/script></body></html>`;
  }

  /* [NO-NAMES 2026-07-29] The presenter chip no longer prints on the slide.
     The name sat top-right of all 46 slides and the audience reads it as part of
     the material; who is speaking is a fact about the SESSION, not about Unit 330,
     and on an exported PDF or a re-used slide it is simply wrong.
     data-presenter is UNTOUCHED — the presenter view and the run-of-show still
     read it, which is where a name belongs. Restore this block to bring them back.
  (function(){
    var CH={'Mario':['mm','MARIO'],'Mauro':['zr','MAURO'],'Lorenzo':['lz','LORENZO']};
    slides.forEach(function(s){
      var p=s.dataset.presenter, c=CH[p]; if(!c) return;
      var el=document.createElement('span'); el.className='pres '+c[0]; el.textContent='\u25b8 '+c[1];
      s.appendChild(el);
    });
  })();
  */
  show(0);
</script>
<script>
  document.querySelectorAll('.carousel').forEach(function(car){
    var imgs=[].slice.call(car.querySelectorAll('.cimg'));
    var dots=[].slice.call(car.querySelectorAll('.cdots b'));
    if(imgs.length===0) return;
    var ci=0, timer=null, interval=parseInt(car.dataset.interval||'4000',10);
    function go(n){ci=(n+imgs.length)%imgs.length;
      imgs.forEach(function(im,k){im.classList.toggle('active',k===ci);});
      dots.forEach(function(d,k){d.classList.toggle('active',k===ci);});}
    dots.forEach(function(d,k){d.onclick=function(){go(k);restart();};});
    function restart(){clearInterval(timer);if(imgs.length>1){timer=setInterval(function(){go(ci+1);},interval);}}
    restart();
  });
</script>
